\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Star at \uc2 Apple\uc2  Is Accused By His Team\b0\par\par\uc2 By Tripp Mickle \par \uc2 1,512 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 August 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Jay Blahnik was a fitness superstar with a book and nearly two decades of work with Nike before he was hired in 2013 to work on the \uc2 Apple\uc2  Watch. He became known inside \uc2 Apple\uc2  as the creator of the watch's signature fitness feature: three circular bands that people could complete through the day by exercising, standing and burning calories. Marketed with the tagline ''Close Your Rings,'' the concept helped galvanize sales of \uc2 Apple\uc2 's first breakout product after \uc2 Steve Jobs\uc2 's death.\par \par \uc2 But along the way, Mr. Blahnik created a toxic work environment, said nine current and former employees who worked with or for Mr. Blahnik and spoke about personnel issues on the condition of anonymity. They said Mr. Blahnik, 57, who leads a roughly 100-person division as vice president for fitness technologies, could be verbally abusive, manipulative and inappropriate. His behavior contributed to decisions by more than 10 workers to seek extended mental health or medical leaves of absence since 2022, about 10 percent of the team, these people said.\par \par \uc2 When confronted with Mr. Blahnik's behavior, \uc2 Apple\uc2  moved to protect him after an internal investigation. The company settled one complaint alleging sexual harassment by Mr. Blahnik and is fighting a lawsuit by an employee, Mandana Mofidi, who said he had bullied her. Mr. Blahnik stayed in his job after company officials said their investigation had found no evidence of wrongdoing, according to interviews and Ms. Mofidi's lawsuit, which she filed against Mr. Blahnik and \uc2 Apple\uc2  last year in Los Angeles County Superior Court.\par \par \uc2 The tension inside Mr. Blahnik's division speaks to workplace dysfunction at the heart of one of \uc2 Apple\uc2 's signature health initiatives. These employees said the company was more willing to protect a star executive than address the concerns of rank-and-file workers.\par \par \uc2 ''At the end of the day, we don't matter,'' said Kayla Desautels, who was the team's social media coordinator before departing last year after a mental health leave. She said she had ''never worked anywhere more toxic.''\par \par \uc2 Lance Lin, an \uc2 Apple\uc2  spokesman, said the company took ''all concerns seriously'' and was ''deeply committed to creating and maintaining a positive and inclusive workplace.'' He said that \uc2 Apple\uc2  thoroughly investigated workplace issues, but that it did not discuss matters involving individual employees, in the interest of respecting their privacy.\par \par \uc2 ''We strongly disagree with the premise of this story, and there are many inaccurate claims and mischaracterizations,'' said Mr. Lin, who declined to provide any specific examples of the inaccuracies when asked to discuss reporting from \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 . He added, ''We will continue to share the facts through the legal process.''\par \par \uc2 In a legal filing, \uc2 Apple\uc2  denied that there had been ''harassment, discrimination, retaliation or any other harm.'' It said Ms. Mofidi had ''received constructive feedback and coaching from her supervisors in response to a well-documented history of unresolved performance issues.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Blahnik did not respond to requests for comment.\par \par \uc2 Around 2017, \uc2 Apple\uc2  asked Mr. Blahnik to develop a subscription service called Apple Fitness+ with workout videos that would cost $9.99 a month. It was launched in 2020 and today offers videos of 12 types of workouts, including dance, Pilates and kickboxing. \uc2 Apple\uc2  has also added audio workouts to inspire people to exercise, including ''Time to Walk'' and ''Time to Run.''\par \par \uc2 Though Mr. Blahnik brought deep experience to the job, he often made unprofessional comments, the nine current and former employees said.\par \par \uc2 During a meeting in 2021 to discuss a fitness feature on the Olympic skier Ted Ligety, Mr. Blahnik joked with colleagues about sleeping with the skier, two people who attended the meeting said. Ms. Mofidi, who wasn't involved in that project, said members of the team had told her later that Mr. Blahnik suggested that a member of the team had gotten Mr. Ligety to do the feature by offering the skier a neck massage. He also spoke about the rear ends and breasts of trainers who were filming workouts, some of the other people who worked on the team said.\par \par \uc2 On another occasion, Mr. Blahnik said in front of several employees that the wife of a Fitness+ manager must have had an affair with another man because the manager's son had a different hair color, Ms. Mofidi and three other former employees said. Mr. Blahnik used a vulgar word in making the remark, they said.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Blahnik would leer at the group's creative director, Wil Tidman, and compliment the way Mr. Tidman dressed, two former employees said. During a work video call with staff, he joked that Mr. Tidman was having an affair with a male producer, they said.\par \par \uc2 One summer night in 2022, Mr. Blahnik sent Mr. Tidman a text message that Mr. Tidman considered inappropriate and unsettling, two people familiar with the message said.\par \par \uc2 After the text message, Mr. Tidman left work on a medical leave, Ms. Mofidi's lawsuit said. Mr. Tidman hired a lawyer and raised the allegation of harassment with \uc2 Apple\uc2 . He didn't file a lawsuit but entered mediation with the company and reached a settlement, the two people familiar with the message said. A lawyer representing Mr. Tidman did not respond to requests for comment.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Mofidi, who had overseen podcasts for the news sites Deadspin and Jezebel, joined \uc2 Apple\uc2  in 2021 to help lead its audio efforts. Mr. Blahnik praised her early work, saying in a review that her first year had been ''marked by significant growth and achievement,'' according to her lawsuit.\par \par \uc2 During that review, Ms. Mofidi raised concerns that she had received a smaller pay increase than her male colleagues, including a direct report, her lawsuit said.\par \par \uc2 The next workday, \uc2 Apple\uc2 's human resources department asked Ms. Mofidi to speak to investigators who were trying to determine whether Mr. Blahnik had harassed Mr. Tidman, her lawsuit said.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Mofidi asked to excuse herself from the inquiry. ''I was aware of his behavior, his vindictiveness,'' she said in an interview. ''I was nervous.'' But the investigator said in an email that ''participation is not voluntary,'' the lawsuit said.\par \par \uc2 Two days after she heard from employee relations, Ms. Mofidi and her team met with Mr. Blahnik about the ''Time to Walk'' workout program, the lawsuit said. When the discussion turned to a guest's failure to settle on music choices in time for an episode, Mr. Blahnik exploded and began yelling at Ms. Mofidi.\par \par \uc2 Kimberly Brooks, a freelancer on the call, told Mr. Blahnik that he didn't need to yell and encouraged him to speak respectfully, the lawsuit said.\par \par \uc2 The behavior ''kicks off this whole campaign that ensues for months,'' Ms. Mofidi, 41, said.\par \par \uc2 One of Mr. Blahnik's lieutenants started criticizing Ms. Mofidi's work. In emails, the lieutenant said she was trying to correct poor performance, the lawsuit said. But Ms. Mofidi thought the emails were an effort to create a record and fire her.\par \par \uc2 ''The whole experience was incredibly isolating, humiliating -- honestly, scary,'' Ms. Mofidi said. Her lawyers said it is unlawful to retaliate against employees who speak to human resources about an employment complaint.\par \par \uc2 Two colleagues, Chris Neil and Nathan Olivarez-Giles, had similar experiences after speaking about Mr. Tidman's harassment claim, said three former employees who learned about those experiences. Mr. Neil and Mr. Olivarez-Giles took mental health breaks before leaving the company, these people said.\par \par \uc2 In early April 2023, Ms. Mofidi said, she was given a choice: She could take a buyout or go on a performance improvement plan, her lawsuit said.\par \par \uc2 The next day, Ms. Mofidi told human resources that she had been targeted because of the sexual harassment review and her concerns about unequal pay. ''I feel a lot of pressure to decide my next move and it's just been thrown at me today,'' she wrote in an email, according to her lawsuit. ''I don't understand why.''\par \par \uc2 Human resources opened an investigation and assured Ms. Mofidi that she would not be harassed further, her lawsuit said. But Ms. Mofidi said the retaliation had continued.\par \par \uc2 ''The idea was to just bully or intimidate me out,'' she said in an interview.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Mofidi received a medical leave after Apple Wellness, the company's health service for employees, found she was experiencing anxiety and depression, her lawsuit said. While she was on leave, employee relations contacted her to say it had spoken with more than 20 other employees but could not confirm that Mr. Blahnik had retaliated, the lawsuit said. The employee relations officials acknowledged that Ms. Mofidi had borne the ''brunt of a lot of the harshness that Jay exhibited'' and said the company should address his behavior, her lawsuit said.\par \par \uc2 When her leave ended, Ms. Mofidi asked to move to another division at \uc2 Apple\uc2  so she could avoid the ''same toxic environment,'' her lawsuit said. Her request was denied, leading her to resign.\par \par \uc2 A trial date for Ms. Mofidi's lawsuit is set for 2027.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250822el8m0003g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
