\par\par \uc2 Foreign Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Air Canada\uc2  and Striking Union Reach Tentative Agreement\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ian Austen \par \uc2 896 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 August 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 5\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The airline planned to restart operations after a strike that disrupted half a million travelers. The agreement would reshape how flight attendants are paid.\par \par \uc2 The agreement the union representing about 10,000 \uc2 Air Canada\uc2  flight attendants reached with the airline on Tuesday not only ended a walkout that affected half a million passengers, it may also lend more muscle to unions facing future back-to-work orders.\par \par \uc2 It could also end a long-disputed pay practice across Canada's airline industry.\par \par \uc2 Air Canada\uc2 , which serves about 130,000 people on 300 flights a day, expected to restore partial service on Tuesday night, but said it would take up to 10 days for normal operations to resume.\par \par \uc2 ''The suspension of our service is extremely difficult for our customers,'' \uc2 Michael Rousseau\uc2 , president and chief executive of \uc2 Air Canada\uc2 , said in a statement. ''Our priority now is to get them moving as quickly as possible.''\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Canadian Union of Public Employees\uc2 , which represents the airline attendants, said the settlement, which emerged from an all-night bargaining session, would bring ''transformational change for our industry.''\par \par \uc2 The agreement came after the union defied two back-to-work orders and rejected the federal government's attempt to force a deal through binding arbitration rather than negotiation. The union's defiance exposed union members and leaders to large fines and even possible jail time.\par \par \uc2 ''We have reclaimed our voice and our power,'' the union said in a statement to its members. ''When our rights were taken away, we stood strong, we fought back.''\par \par \uc2 The tentative deal, while a significant step, must still be approved by flight attendants, who overwhelmingly supported the strike that began on Saturday.\par \par \uc2 The union said that the deal was reached at 4:23 a.m. Eastern time, after slightly more than nine hours of talks and with the help of a mediator.\par \par \uc2 ''We must fully cooperate with the resumption of operations,'' the union's bargaining committee said in a message to its members.\par \par \uc2 The overnight talks yielded a significant gain for flight attendants.\par \par \uc2 The union had demanded that \uc2 Air Canada\uc2  follow the lead of some U.S. airlines by paying attendants for work that they do before takeoff and after landing, like boarding passengers and carrying out safety checks.\par \par \uc2 Like many airlines, \uc2 Air Canada\uc2  pays flight attendants only for work done while the plane is in the air.\par \par \uc2 During earlier rounds of negotiation, \uc2 Air Canada\uc2  had agreed to pay attendants for ground work, but at a lower hourly pay rate and for a limited amount of time, according to the union.\par \par \uc2 A person familiar with the agreement, who was not authorized to publicly disclose the terms before they were shared with flight attendants, said \uc2 Air Canada\uc2  had agreed to pay 70 percent of the regular wage rate for 45 minutes of ground work on smaller aircraft and for 60 minutes on larger planes.\par \par \uc2 ''Unpaid work is over,'' the union said.\par \par \uc2 The formula differs from those used at American air carriers that pay for work before and after flights. Flight attendants at American and Delta are paid half their hourly rate for however long boarding takes.\par \par \uc2 A different branch of the \uc2 Canadian Union of Public Employees\uc2  represents flight attendants at \uc2 WestJet\uc2 , \uc2 Air Canada\uc2 's largest domestic competitor, and \uc2 Porter Airlines\uc2 , a Toronto-based carrier. The union is now likely to demand that those two carriers match \uc2 Air Canada\uc2  on ground pay in future contract talks.\par \par \uc2 The union also wanted broader wage increases. It said \uc2 Air Canada\uc2  had offered to increase wages by 17.2 percent over four years, an amount the union said would not keep pace with inflation.\par \par \uc2 The person familiar with the agreement said \uc2 Air Canada\uc2  had increased its wage offer, but would not disclose the amount.\par \par \uc2 The labor dispute was the latest in a series of walkouts the federal government has shut down through arbitration.\par \par \uc2 It became the tool of choice after a \uc2 Supreme Court of Canada\uc2  ruling in 2015 that upheld the constitutional right to strike and limited the ability of governments to interfere even in widely disruptive walkouts, said Stephanie Ross, an associate professor of labor studies, at \uc2 McMaster University\uc2  in Hamilton, Ontario.\par \par \uc2 This time, however, the union defied the back-to-work orders arguing that they were unconstitutional and asked the Federal Court of Canada to strike down both the back-to-work orders and the arbitration. An arbitrated negotiation rarely produces new contract provisions, like for ground pay.\par \par \uc2 The union's aggressive stance makes it less likely that any government will again attempt to shut down a strike through arbitration, said David J. Doorey, a professor of workplace law at \uc2 York University in Toronto\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''The Canadian labor movement has drawn a line in the sand and said that it won't tolerate that practice anymore,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 The talks between the union and \uc2 Air Canada\uc2  restarted after \uc2 Patty Hajdu\uc2 , Canada's labor minister, said she had started an investigation into the issue of unpaid ground work, calling the practice ''deeply disturbing'' in an interview with \uc2 CBC News\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''We're going to hold the government to their word on this,'' Hugh Pouliot, a spokesman for the union, said. ''It's a systemic problem that requires a systemic solution.''\par \par \uc2 Niraj Chokshi contributed reporting from New York.\par \par \uc2 Niraj Chokshi contributed reporting from New York.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250820el8k0004b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
