\par\par \uc2 Foreign Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Attendants To Soon Get New Benefit: Boarding Pay\b0\par\par\uc2 By Niraj Chokshi \par \uc2 731 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 August 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 5\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The strike, which ended on Tuesday, focused attention on why many airlines don't start paying flight attendants until the plane doors are shut.\par \par \uc2 Flight attendants for \uc2 Air Canada\uc2  ended a three-day strike on Tuesday, allowing the airline to slowly restart flying.\par \par \uc2 Details about the deal were sparse, but the flight attendants union suggested that it had secured a benefit that had become a big focus of contract talks: pay for work done on the ground before and after flights take off and land.\par \par \uc2 ''Unpaid work is over,'' the union, the \uc2 Canadian Union of Public Employees\uc2 , which represents more than 10,000 flight attendants at \uc2 Air Canada\uc2  and a subsidiary, said in a brief statement. ''We have reclaimed our voice and our power.''\par \par \uc2 The strike, which began on Saturday, resulting in more than 2,000 flight cancellations, had brought fresh attention to a common airline pay practice. Many airlines have for years based flight attendants' pay on how many hours they work when the cabin doors are closed, not including the time it takes to get passengers on and off planes.\par \par \uc2 How do other airlines handle boarding pay?\par \par \uc2 For decades, most flight attendants in North America were not paid for boarding. In 2022, \uc2 Delta Air Lines\uc2  became the first major U.S. carrier to pay employees for that time, a change it introduced during a campaign to unionize the company's flight attendants. \uc2 American Airlines\uc2  and \uc2 Alaska Airlines\uc2 , where attendants were represented by unions, have since started paying for boarding, too.\par \par \uc2 But boarding pay remains uncommon. \uc2 United Airlines\uc2  has agreed to offer boarding pay in contract talks with flight attendants taking place now.\par \par \uc2 And on Monday, flight attendants for \uc2 PSA Airlines\uc2 , an \uc2 American Airlines\uc2  subsidiary, protested at various U.S. airports, calling for better wages and boarding pay. Regional carriers like PSA operate shorter flights, meaning flight attendants there most likely spend a greater share of their time on duty during boarding than at other airlines.\par \par \uc2 At American, Alaska and Delta, flight attendants are paid about half of their hourly rate during boarding. \uc2 Air Canada\uc2  agreed to match that in its latest offer to the flight attendants, but the union criticized that proposal, saying it excluded some types of work.\par \par \uc2 How are delayed and canceled flight handled?\par \par \uc2 The rules for compensating flight attendants for delayed and canceled trips vary by airline.\par \par \uc2 Many flight attendants who are represented by unions are guaranteed at least some pay if they report for duty and a trip is canceled or is delayed for a long time. Companies typically try to reassign flight attendants in such cases, too. If a trip is canceled with more advanced notice, though, a flight attendant may receive little or no compensation at some airlines.\par \par \uc2 At American, if passengers board a flight that is later canceled, flight attendants are still paid for boarding. If passengers are asked to leave the plane and then return, flight attendants receive boarding pay for each time passengers are let on the plane.\par \par \uc2 Why has boarding pay been introduced only recently?\par \par \uc2 Boarding pay is fairly new in part because flight attendants have not had enough bargaining power in recent decades to get airlines to agree to it.\par \par \uc2 The Sept. 11, 2001, terrorist attacks dealt a huge financial blow to airlines that lasted for years. A decade later, several airlines were forced to seek bankruptcy protection or sell themselves to other companies.\par \par \uc2 During those tumultuous years, flight attendants, pilots and other workers were generally fighting to protect existing benefits, revive ones that had been taken away or eke out small improvements.\par \par \uc2 In the years since the coronavirus pandemic, however, many airlines have done incredibly well financially, emboldening labor unions to demand better pay and benefits.\par \par \uc2 Boarding has also become more onerous than it once was. Planes are more consistently crowded. Airlines expect flights to move more quickly. And passengers have started bringing more carry-on bags to avoid the checked-bag fees that many airlines have introduced in recent decades. An increase in disruptive passengers early in the recovery from the pandemic made that work even more stressful.\par \par \uc2 ''It can be the most chaotic part of the day,'' said Paul Hartshorn, a flight attendant and spokesman for the \uc2 Association of Professional Flight Attendants\uc2 , the union that represents flight attendants at \uc2 American Airlines\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250820el8k0003s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
