\par\par \uc2 Foreign Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Air Canada\uc2  Strike That Grounded Hundreds of Flights Is Shut Down\b0\par\par\uc2 By Matina Stevis-Gridneff and Ian Austen \par \uc2 875 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 August 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A walkout by flight attendants ended after about 12 hours with the imposition of binding arbitration. But getting travel back to normal will take days.\par \par \uc2 Just under 12 hours after \uc2 Air Canada\uc2 's 10,000 flight attendants walked off the job, seeking better wages, the government imposed binding arbitration to shut down the strike and avert mounting travel chaos.\par \par \uc2 ''The impact of the work stoppage at \uc2 Air Canada\uc2  that began early this morning is already being felt by travelers,'' \uc2 Patty Hajdu\uc2 , the labor minister in the Liberal government, told reporters. ''This is causing significant harm and has negative impacts on Canadians and the Canadian economy.''\par \par \uc2 Air Canada\uc2  will not immediately begin flying, she said. An independent industrial labor relations board will need 24 to 48 hours for a review before issuing a back-to-work order. Ms. Hajdu said that \uc2 Air Canada\uc2  has told the government that restoring full service would take an additional five to 10 days.\par \par \uc2 In an unsigned email, \uc2 Air Canada\uc2 , which said that it had locked out the striking workers early Saturday, declined to comment on the minister's decision, adding that it was too early to say how long it would take to get its planes flying.\par \par \uc2 Hundreds of striking flight attendants outside Toronto Pearson International Airport reacted swiftly and angrily, chanting, ''Shame on you, \uc2 Patty Hajdu\uc2 ,'' and ''We won't stop,'' as passing motorists honked their horns in solidarity.\par \par \uc2 ''The Liberals are violating our charter rights to take job action and giving \uc2 Air Canada\uc2  exactly what they want -- hours and hours of unpaid labor from underpaid flight attendants,'' \uc2 Wesley Lesosky\uc2 , the president of the \uc2 Air Canada\uc2  group of the \uc2 Canadian Union of Public Employees\uc2 , said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 The flight attendants, 70 percent of whom are women, are seeking improved compensation, particularly for the hours they are working but not airborne. Currently, \uc2 Air Canada\uc2  flight attendants are paid an hourly wage only for the time planes are in the air, a formula they say is unfair. In recent years, some major U.S. airlines, including \uc2 Delta Air Lines\uc2 , \uc2 American Airlines\uc2  and \uc2 Alaska Airlines\uc2 , have started paying for work performed while planes are on the ground.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Hajdu said that the airline and negotiators had met through Friday night into Saturday morning. While an offer was made by one of the parties, she said, talks collapsed.\par \par \uc2 Earlier in the week, \uc2 Air Canada\uc2  asked the government to force the union to enter arbitration after failing to get the union to agree to the process. Government-mandated arbitration, however, is normally used to end long and disruptive walkouts, and arbitrators typically do not introduce major changes -- like compensation for work before and after flights -- in contracts they impose.\par \par \uc2 Though short, the strike had already created difficulties, both for those trying to reach some of the 65 foreign countries \uc2 Air Canada\uc2  flies to, and for domestic travelers, since the country's vastness often makes flying the only viable form of travel. The airline said it had canceled about 700 flights, expected to affect about 130,000 passengers daily.\par \par \uc2 Rob Van Helden of the Netherlands had been vacationing in Costa Rica as part of a group of 16 family friends -- eight of them children -- and was flying to Amsterdam through Toronto when the strike began.\par \par \uc2 The group stayed at a hotel Friday, and some of the adults were at Pearson airport early Saturday, before an end to the strike was announced, trying to work out their options. \uc2 Air Canada\uc2  had booked them on a flight on Wednesday, he said, which meant four more nights of hotel bills in Canada.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Van Helden said the group was looking into paying about $600 a person to fly to Detroit and then to Amsterdam on KLM, the Dutch carrier.\par \par \uc2 Air Canada\uc2  said it would try to rebook passengers on other carriers, but the busy summer travel period and a lack of capacity on its smaller domestic competitors was making that hard.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Hajdu cited the economic disruption President Trump's tariffs have brought to Canada's economy as one of the reasons behind her decision to end the strike and lockout through arbitration. She noted, however, that nothing prevents the airline and union from still reaching a negotiated agreement.\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 Supreme Court of Canada\uc2  ruling about a decade ago affirmed the right of workers to strike even when their actions might be disruptive. But the federal government has sometimes followed the path taken by Ms. Hajdu and used arbitration to thwart or end walkouts.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  have already challenged the government's use of arbitration, said David J. Doorey, a professor of workplace law at \uc2 York University in Toronto\uc2 . He predicted that the courts were likely to rule against the government.\par \par \uc2 The use of arbitration is widely condemned by unions, but Ms. Hajdu rejected suggestions that the government is ''anti-union.''\par \par \uc2 ''We believe that unions are an essential part of a healthy and growing economy,'' she said, adding that in the case of this strike ''it is clear that they are at an impasse, and it is very clear they need some help in arbitrating the final items.''\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250817el8h0005c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
