\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Covid Subway Crews To Receive Back Pay\b0\par\par\uc2 By Stefanos Chen and Liam Stack \par \uc2 1,023 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 August 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 17\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A $3 million settlement will be divided among 452 underpaid workers employed at the height of the coronavirus pandemic, the city comptroller's office said.\par \par \uc2 At the height of the coronavirus pandemic, Susana Baez would begin her shift at 1 a.m., scrubbing trash, vomit and human excrement from subway cars.\par \par \uc2 She and her team, a largely immigrant work force contracted by private companies to clean New York City's subway stations and train cars, often had to work without enough safety equipment, like gloves, she said. Many contracted Covid-19 on the job.\par \par \uc2 ''It was trauma,'' Ms. Baez, 53, said in Spanish about the job, which she performed from 2020 to 2023, when her contract abruptly ended.\par \par \uc2 Now, Ms. Baez and more than 450 other subway cleaners will split $3 million in back pay, after a multiyear investigation by the city comptroller found that they were grossly underpaid.\par \par \uc2 The workers, who were employed by two private cleaning companies, earned around 25 percent less than they were owed, said Brad Lander, the city comptroller. His office sets the prevailing wage, or the typical rate, for certain types of public work.\par \par \uc2 The cleaners made $16 to $18 an hour on average in the first years of the pandemic, without supplemental benefits, when $20 to $21 an hour was standard, Mr. Lander said. Minimum wage at the time was $15 an hour.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Lander's office sued the cleaning companies, LN Pro Services and Fleetwash, last year for failing to meet the standard. On Tuesday, he announced settlements that could net the workers an average of more than $6,600 in back pay, depending on their length of service, with some cleaners expected to receive more than $20,000.\par \par \uc2 ''These 452 workers risked their health and safety and their lives to clean subway cars, to give New Yorkers confidence that they could ride them in our darkest hour,'' Mr. Lander said. ''They deserve to be protected and not cheated.''\par \par \uc2 The bulk of the money will be paid by the \uc2 Metropolitan Transportation Authority\uc2 , the state agency that runs the subway and hired the cleaning companies, the comptroller's office said.\par \par \uc2 John McCarthy, the chief of policy and external relations at the M.T.A., said the transit authority did not acknowledge any intentional wrongdoing by the contractors but would not further discuss the settlements.\par \par \uc2 During the pandemic's darkest days, the cleaners were classified as essential workers, or those whose jobs were critical for the functioning of society, including medical workers, grocery store clerks and laborers who kept infrastructure like the subway up and running.\par \par \uc2 As many other New Yorkers safely worked from home -- and as some left New York City entirely -- these essential workers toiled in dangerous conditions.\par \par \uc2 The settlements were reached in late July, after the comptroller's office argued that the contracted cleaners should have been paid at the same scale as cleaning staff assigned to similar public service projects.\par \par \uc2 The cleaning companies signed contracts with the M.T.A. in 2020, when \uc2 Andrew M. Cuomo\uc2 , then the governor, directed the transit agency to disinfect subway cars at a number of train station terminals overnight. It was one of the few times in the famously 24-hour transit service's history that the subway system closed for several hours a day.\par \par \uc2 But some of the contractors said they were paid as little as half as much as M.T.A. employees who did the same work before the pandemic, and often without access to health insurance, at a time when the virus was surging.\par \par \uc2 The comptroller's office directed the M.T.A. to pay the workers more at the time, but the transit agency, which was controlled by Mr. Cuomo, argued that the contracts fell outside the scope of work that required prevailing wages.\par \par \uc2 James Parrott, a labor expert and a senior adviser at the Center for New York City Affairs at the New School, said the settlements were a long time coming, and that the transit agency's delay reflects poorly on Mr. Cuomo, who lost the Democratic primary for mayor in June and is now running as an independent candidate in the general election.\par \par \uc2 ''It's indicative of a lack of sufficient regard that Cuomo and the M.T.A. leadership under his administration had for workers' rights across the board,'' Mr. Parrott said.\par \par \uc2 During a mayoral debate in June, Mr. Cuomo and Mr. Lander, who also ran in the primary and finished in third place behind Mr. Cuomo, briefly clashed over the case.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Lander said Mr. Cuomo had ''cheated'' the subway cleaners, but Mr. Cuomo replied that the specifics of their employment and compensation had been determined by the M.T.A.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, Rich Azzopardi, a spokesman for Mr. Cuomo, said that as governor, Mr. Cuomo had been committed to ensuring that people who worked for the state were paid the prevailing wage.\par \par \uc2 ''No one fought harder to pass and uphold prevailing wage laws than \uc2 Andrew Cuomo\uc2 ,'' Mr. Azzopardi said. ''If they were doing work for the M.T.A., workers have to be paid prevailing wage and must be paid every penny they are owed.''\par \par \uc2 For Ms. Baez, who stopped cleaning subways after her contract was not renewed, the settlements bring some closure.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Baez, who lives in Queens and is originally from the Dominican Republic, said she had paired her shifts as a subway cleaner with a job as a home attendant to make ends meet.\par \par \uc2 While working as a cleaner, she was also dealing with cervical cancer, and the fear of catching Covid-19 was constant, she said. Ms. Baez contracted the virus early in the pandemic and missed a month of work.\par \par \uc2 The workers will receive their share of the settlement funds by the end of November, said Chloe Chik, a spokeswoman for the comptroller.\par \par \uc2 Though she does not know how much money she will receive, Ms. Baez welcomed the news.\par \par \uc2 ''Whatever it is,'' she said, ''we deserve it.''\par \par \uc2 Taylor Robinson contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Taylor Robinson contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250813el8d0004b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
