\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Thousands of \uc2 Boeing\uc2  Workers Who Make Fighter Jets Walk Off the Job\b0\par\par\uc2 By Cade Metz \par \uc2 438 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 August 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 About 3,200 machinists in St. Louis walk off the job after failing to reach an agreement on wages and retirement benefits.\par \par \uc2 About 3,200 machinists who build \uc2 Boeing\uc2  fighter jets in the St. Louis area walked off the job on Monday, after their union failed to reach an agreement with the aircraft manufacturer.\par \par \uc2 The strike, which the union said began at midnight, was the first in nearly three decades for the local chapter of the \uc2 International Association of Machinists and Aerospace Workers\uc2 , IAM District 837.\par \par \uc2 In announcing the strike, the union rejected the increase to wages and retirement contributions that the company was offering. When the union last went on strike, in 1996, the work stoppage lasted 99 days.\par \par \uc2 ''IAM District 837 members have spoken loud and clear, they deserve a contract that reflects their skill, dedication, and the critical role they play in our nation's defense,'' Tom Boelling, the top official at the union's St. Louis chapter, said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 Last week, \uc2 Boeing\uc2  reported $22.7 billion in revenue for its latest financial quarter, the company's largest quarterly revenue figure in six years. But the strike could put added pressure on the company, which generates about 30 percent of its revenue from its defense and space division.\par \par \uc2 Even with its impressive revenue haul, \uc2 Boeing\uc2  reported a loss of $612 million in the quarter, as it continues to battle back from a series of damaging incidents over the last several years. Last year, \uc2 Boeing\uc2  faced a crisis when a poorly installed panel blew off a \uc2 Boeing\uc2  737 Max jet during a flight. And the Max was involved in two fatal crashes in 2018 and 2019.\par \par \uc2 ''We're disappointed our employees rejected an offer that featured 40 percent average wage growth and resolved their primary issue on alternative work schedules,'' Dan Gillian, vice president of Boeing Air Dominance and general manager and senior executive at the St. Louis site, said in an emailed statement.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Gillian said \uc2 Boeing\uc2  had prepared for the work stoppage and ''have fully implemented our contingency plan to ensure our nonstriking work force can continue supporting our customers.''\par \par \uc2 Members of the St. Louis local help build fighter aircraft such as the F-15 and the T-7 training jet while also manufacturing components for \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's 777X commercial jets.\par \par \uc2 Last year, a two-month strike brought production of the Max to a near standstill. But that strike involved 30,000 employees, significantly more than the number threatening to strike in St. Louis.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250805el8500035\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
