\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Trump Pushes Out Labor Data Chief Over Jobs Report\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ben Casselman and Tony Romm \par \uc2 1,602 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 August 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Economists said ousting the head of the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  could undermine confidence in government economic data.\par \par \uc2 President Trump unleashed his fury about weakness in the labor market on Friday, saying without evidence that the data were ''rigged'' and that he was firing the Senate-confirmed Department of Labor official responsible for pulling together the numbers each month.\par \par \uc2 In a long post on social media, Mr. Trump said he had directed his team to fire Erika McEntarfer, the commissioner of the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2 , who was confirmed on a bipartisan basis in 2024.\par \par \uc2 Emily Liddel, an associate commissioner for the bureau, confirmed late Friday that Dr. McEntarfer had been fired and that William Wiatrowski, the deputy commissioner, would serve as acting commissioner.\par \par \uc2 The president fired Dr. McEntarfer after the bureau released monthly jobs data showing surprisingly weak hiring in July and large downward revisions to job growth in the previous two months. Economists widely interpreted the report as evidence that Mr. Trump's policies were beginning to take a toll on the economy, though the president insisted in a subsequent post that the country was ''doing GREAT!''\par \par \uc2 Lori Chavez-DeRemer, the labor secretary, echoed Mr. Trump's concerns about Dr. McEntarfer in a post on social media.\par \par \uc2 ''So you know what I did?'' Mr. Trump later told reporters, as he claimed the numbers were ''phony.'' ''I fired her, and you know what? I did the right thing.''\par \par \uc2 Dr. McEntarfer was appointed to her post by President \uc2 Joseph R. Biden Jr.\uc2  in 2023 after a long career at the Census Bureau and other agencies, where she served under presidents of both parties, including Mr. Trump. Among the Republicans who voted to confirm her as commissioner was Vice President \uc2 JD Vance\uc2 , who was then an Ohio senator.\par \par \uc2 The firing prompted swift criticism from economists, former government officials and others, who said the removal would further erode trust in government statistics and make it more difficult for policymakers, investors and businesses, who rely on having dependable data about the economy to make decisions. In addition to the monthly jobs numbers, the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  is responsible for producing data on inflation, wages and other aspects of the economy.\par \par \uc2 William W. Beach, who led the bureau during Mr. Trump's first term, criticized the move to fire Dr. McEntarfer on Friday.\par \par \uc2 ''It's unfortunate,'' he said. ''This could set a precedent where bad news on many different fronts is a reason for dismissing a person.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Beach, who was appointed by Mr. Trump in 2019 and remained in the role for the first two years of the Biden administration, said he had never felt pressure to manipulate the data under either president. Even if there were such pressure, he said, there is ''no way'' the commissioner could interfere in the revisions process, which is conducted by career employees.\par \par \uc2 Erica Groshen\uc2 , who led the agency under President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 , called the decision ''a terrible precedent.''\par \par \uc2 ''I hope will be reversed because it undermines the integrity of our statistical system and really all of government data and science,'' she added, calling it ''a very sad day.''\par \par \uc2 Dr. McEntarfer's tenure got off to a rough start last year when the agency made a series of missteps in which Wall Street firms had access to data before the general public. But none of those incidents involved issues with the statistics themselves.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump and his top aides have made a habit of attacking government agencies, researchers and watchdogs when they have produced findings that the president does not like. That has led to concerns that Mr. Trump could seek to interfere with the operations of the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  and other statistical agencies, particularly if the economy begins to take a turn for the worse.\par \par \uc2 Until now, however, most experts on the statistical system said they remained confident in the data produced by the agencies and had seen no evidence of political interference in their operations. Current and former agency staff members consistently echoed that message -- in part, they said, because they trusted Dr. McEntarfer and her counterparts at the other major statistical agencies to protect their independence.\par \par \uc2 ''If that pressure got too great, you would see people resigning rather than shape the numbers,'' Mr. Beach said.\par \par \uc2 Economists across the ideological spectrum said Mr. Trump's move to oust Dr. McEntarfer was likely to erode public confidence in the data published by the administration.\par \par \uc2 ''If you want people to stop trusting the numbers coming out of the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2 , firing the person who is confirmed by the Senate to make sure those numbers are trustworthy is a real good way to do it,'' said \uc2 Martha Gimbel\uc2 , the executive director of the Budget Lab at Yale, who served in the White House under Mr. Biden.\par \par \uc2 Dr. McEntarfer could not immediately be reached for comment.\par \par \uc2 On Friday morning, the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  released data showing that employers added only 73,000 new jobs in July. It also notably revised data for the previous two months, reducing the number of jobs created by 258,000. While revisions to previous months are common, it was an unusually high number that came as a surprise. It suggested the labor market was not as resilient as it had seemed earlier this summer.\par \par \uc2 Shortly after the numbers were released, Stephen Miran, the chair of the \uc2 White House Council of Economic Advisers\uc2 , offered an explanation for the jobs revision that was much different from Mr. Trump's.\par \par \uc2 On CNBC, he said much of the change was the result of ''quirks in the seasonal adjustment process'' and even the president's own policies, particularly on immigration, potentially affecting hiring numbers for May and June. He made no mention of any concerns about manipulated data as he sought to recast the slowdown in July as a ''pretty decent'' jobs report.\par \par \uc2 By evening, \uc2 Kevin Hassett\uc2 , the director of the National Economic Council, sought to frame the firing as an attempt to restore ''trust'' at the statistics agency. Unlike Mr. Trump, who described the revisions as politically motivated, Mr. Hassett said its jobs figures had been ''awful'' for some time.\par \par \uc2 ''I think it is a good time for a fresh set of eyes to look at what the heck is going on,'' he told Fox Business.\par \par \uc2 In his social media posts on Friday, Mr. Trump provided no evidence that Dr. McEntarfer had injected political bias into her agency's data. And his criticisms contained contradictions and inaccuracies.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump complained about not just the latest jobs numbers but also a set of revisions from last year. The bureau, like other statistical agencies, routinely updates its figures to incorporate data that wasn't initially available or to reflect information from more authoritative sources.\par \par \uc2 Last August, the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  said employers had added roughly 818,000 fewer jobs over a 12-month period than previously believed. That announcement was part of a normal annual revision process, although the change was unusually large. (It was also preliminary -- the final figures were revised down by just under 600,000 jobs.)\par \par \uc2 In a social media post on Friday, Mr. Trump said the revision was made ''right after the election.'' In fact, the announcement was made roughly two and a half months before Election Day. Indeed, Mr. Trump posted about the revisions at the time, calling them a ''MASSIVE SCANDAL.''\par \par \uc2 To the agency's defenders, however, the twin revisions show that it operates without political bias and was willing to announce politically inconvenient news under presidents of both parties.\par \par \uc2 ''President Trump is completely wrong in asserting there's been any sort of anti-Trump bias in the labor market data,'' said \uc2 Michael Strain\uc2 , an economist at the conservative \uc2 American Enterprise Institute\uc2 . ''I think that assertion is wholly unsupported.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Strain said that government data is revised frequently, and that doing so reflected a ''standard'' practice to ensure its quality. In this case, he acknowledged that the change was ''historically large'' but ''doesn't smell fishy.''\par \par \uc2 Federal statistical agencies have faced mounting challenges in recent years as Americans have become more reluctant to respond to the surveys that are the basis for much of the nation's economic data. Shrinking budgets have made it harder to make up for falling response rates, and to develop new approaches to replace surveys altogether.\par \par \uc2 Those concerns predate the current administration, but have grown worse since Mr. Trump returned to office. The statistical agencies have struggled with staff attrition as a result of the president's freeze on federal hiring, combined with the buyouts he offered early in his term. The president's budget also proposed further staff and funding cuts.\par \par \uc2 In June, the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  said it was reducing its collection of data on consumer prices in response to resource constraints. Economists warned that, over time, such cuts could erode the reliability of the inflation data that \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  policymakers rely on when setting interest rates, and that determine cost-of-living increases in union contracts and Social Security benefits, among other uses.\par \par \uc2 Asked about those cuts on Wednesday, \uc2 Jerome H. Powell\uc2 , the Fed chair, said policymakers were ''getting the data that we need to do our jobs.'' But he stressed the importance of the federal statistical agencies.\par \par \uc2 ''The government data is really the gold standard in data,'' he said. ''We need it to be good and to be able to rely on it.''\par \par \uc2 Sydney Ember contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Sydney Ember contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250802el820002v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
