\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Boeing\uc2  Bounces Back With Strongest Report Of Revenue in 6 Years\b0\par\par\uc2 By Niraj Chokshi \par \uc2 809 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 July 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 6\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Although the aerospace company lost money in the second quarter, it built and sold more planes as it recovered from quality crises and a workers strike.\par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2  reported $22.7 billion in revenue in the three months that ended in June, its biggest quarterly haul in six years, an indication of the progress the company has made in producing and delivering commercial planes after a series of damaging crises.\par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2  still reported a loss of $612 million in the quarter, but that was a substantial improvement over the $1.4 billion loss during the same period a year earlier.\par \par \uc2 ''We're just over halfway through 2025, and I'm pleased with our progress,'' \uc2 Kelly Ortberg\uc2 , the company's chief executive, said in a message to employees. ''Change takes time, but we're starting to see a difference in our performance across the business.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Ortberg, a longtime aerospace executive, joined the company last August as it sought to recover from its latest crisis, caused by a poorly installed panel that blew off a \uc2 Boeing\uc2  737 Max jet during a flight early last year. No one was killed, but the episode resurfaced widespread concerns about \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's planes five years after two fatal crashes involving the Max.\par \par \uc2 The new chief executive ordered changes aimed at improving quality and safety. And the company has steadily increased production and deliveries of its passenger planes since. \uc2 Boeing\uc2  delivered 280 commercial jets in the first half of this year, the most in the first six months of any year since 2018, before the fatal Max crashes.\par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2  has also increased production of the Max to 38 planes per month, a ceiling the \uc2 Federal Aviation Administration\uc2  imposed after the panel blowout. Mr. Ortberg said the company was planning to ask the F.A.A. for permission to increase production to 42 Max jets a month when internal quality metrics indicated that it was safe to do so. The company has also increased production of the 787 Dreamliner, a larger, twin-aisle plane, to seven jets per month.\par \par \uc2 New orders for the company's planes have improved, too, with more than 420 commercial jets ordered in the second quarter. That was its best quarterly sales since late 2023. Those orders may have been boosted by trade deals struck by President Trump, several of which included commitments from other countries to buy \uc2 Boeing\uc2  planes, though experts said many such orders might have been placed anyway.\par \par \uc2 During Mr. Trump's visit to the Middle East in May, \uc2 Qatar Airways\uc2  announced plans to buy as many as 210 of \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's bigger, twin-aisle jets, the 787 Dreamliner and 777-9, which has yet to be certified to carry passengers. It was the largest order \uc2 Boeing\uc2  has received for such planes. In all, the company said, it received orders for 5,900 planes, a value of $522 billion.\par \par \uc2 The company's share price was up about 1.5 percent in early trading on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 In addition to the improvements in production and sales, \uc2 Boeing\uc2  and the rest of the aerospace industry scored a major victory this week when the United States and the \uc2 European Union\uc2  reached a trade deal that spared planes and their parts from tariffs.\par \par \uc2 The company was again thrust into the spotlight in June when a 787 Dreamliner operated by \uc2 Air India\uc2  crashed, killing 260 people. But a preliminary report from the Indian authorities focused on the movement of switches that control the supply of fuel to the plane's engines, which experts said were probably moved by one of the pilots -- intentionally or accidentally. Bryan Bedford, the newly appointed F.A.A. administrator, also recently reportedly said he had a ''high level of confidence'' that there was no mechanical problem with the plane's fuel control switches.\par \par \uc2 Despite its recent progress, \uc2 Boeing\uc2  has challenges ahead, including whether it can sustain and increase the pace of production and avoid any further crises. Mr. Ortberg also said on Tuesday that he now expected certification of the smallest and largest variants of the Max -- the Max 7 and Max 10 -- to slip into next year because a technical fix for the engine's anti-icing system was taking longer than expected.\par \par \uc2 Last year, a two-month strike brought production of the Max to a near standstill. The company is now facing the prospect of another strike, this time by workers in Missouri and Illinois, who work on fighter jets and other military programs. More than 3,200 of those workers, who are represented by the \uc2 International Association of Machinists and Aerospace Workers\uc2 , voted to reject the company's latest contract offer on Sunday, when the current contract expired. That vote started a one-week countdown to the start of a strike.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250730el7u0003u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
