\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Changes Are Planned for Skilled-Worker Visas to Focus on Higher Wages\b0\par\par\uc2 By Hamed Aleaziz \par \uc2 729 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 July 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 17\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 In an interview with \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 , the new director of \uc2 U.S. Citizenship and Immigration Services\uc2  said the test to become a U.S. citizen was too easy.\par \par \uc2 The Trump administration is planning to change the visa system for skilled foreign workers, a program at the center of a dispute between immigration hard-liners and tech industry leaders, said the new director of \uc2 U.S. Citizenship and Immigration Services\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In an interview with \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 , \uc2 Joseph Edlow\uc2 , the director of U.S.C.I.S., also said the test to become a U.S. citizen was too easy and should change.\par \par \uc2 ''The test as it's laid out right now, it's not very difficult,'' Mr. Edlow said on Thursday. ''It's very easy to kind of memorize the answers. I don't think we're really comporting with the spirit of the law.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Edlow illuminated how the agency at the heart of the country's immigration system would operate in President Trump's second term, at a moment when the president has ordered a sweeping crackdown on immigration and a mass deportation campaign.\par \par \uc2 The H-1B visa program for foreign workers has been the subject of a fierce debate within the Republican Party. Mr. Edlow said it should favor companies that plan to pay foreign workers higher wages.\par \par \uc2 The proposed changes to the system could help alleviate criticism from those in the right wing of the Republican party who say the program brings in workers who are willing to accept lower salaries than American workers. This week, Vice President \uc2 JD Vance\uc2  criticized companies who lay off their own employees and then hire foreign workers.\par \par \uc2 But some of Mr. Trump's most prominent backers in the tech industry have said they rely on the program because they can't find enough qualified American workers.\par \par \uc2 ''I really do think that the way H-1B needs to be used, and this is one of my favorite phrases, is to, along with a lot of other parts of immigration, supplement, not supplant, U.S. economy and U.S. businesses and U.S. workers,'' Mr. Edlow said.\par \par \uc2 Historically, 85,000 visas are provided to hire so-called high-skilled foreign workers at companies through a lottery process. Mr. Edlow's proposed changes would have to be approved through the federal government's rule-making process.\par \par \uc2 Doug Rand, a former Biden administration official, said changing the H1-B process to favor higher-wage earners was misguided.\par \par \uc2 ''Like it or not, the H-1B program is the main way that U.S. companies can hire the best and brightest international graduates of U.S. universities, and Congress never allowed D.H.S. to put its thumb on the scale based on salary,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Edlow also said the administration wanted to change the naturalization test required by prospective U.S. citizens.\par \par \uc2 As of now, immigrants study 100 civics questions and then must respond correctly to six out of 10 questions to pass that portion of the test. During the first Trump administration, the agency increased the number of questions and required applicants to respond correctly to 12 out of 20 questions.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Edlow says the agency plans to return to a version of that test soon.\par \par \uc2 After a brief tenure leading U.S.C.I.S. in an acting capacity in 2020, Mr. Edlow has now been confirmed by the Senate to lead the agency with a hand in citizenship certification and work visas, and the refugee and asylum apparatus.\par \par \uc2 ''I think it absolutely should be a net positive,'' Mr. Edlow said of immigration into America. ''And if we're looking at the people that are coming over, that are especially coming over to advance certain economic agendas that we have and otherwise benefit the national interest -- that's absolutely what we need to be taking care of.''\par \par \uc2 In the first Trump administration, U.S.C.I.S. made it more difficult for immigrants who use public benefits to get permanent resident cards, known as green cards (Mr. Edlow said he did not plan to revive this measure). The agency's asylum officers contended with changes to the asylum system -- to restrict protections -- that were, at times, blocked by federal courts.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250726el7q00049\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
