\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 As \uc2 European Union\uc2  Nears Deal With U.S., It Is Ready to Retaliate\b0\par\par\uc2 By Liz Alderman \par \uc2 1,034 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 July 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Nearly every member of the \uc2 European Union\uc2  supports a plan to hit back against President Trump's tariffs if no agreement is reached.\par \par \uc2 European Union\uc2  officials are voicing optimism about a possible trade deal with the United States, aiming for 15 percent across-the-board tariffs, including on cars and car parts, but they have also made clear that they are prepared to strike back should an agreement fall through.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, nearly every \uc2 European Union\uc2  country voted to back a plan to retaliate against President Trump's tariffs, setting the stage to hit more than 93 billion euros' worth of U.S. imports with levies should he follow through on his threat to tax European imports at 30 percent. Hungary was the lone holdout.\par \par \uc2 The measures will kick in on Aug. 7 unless a satisfactory accord is reached, Olof Gill, a spokesman for the \uc2 European Commission\uc2 , the \uc2 European Union\uc2 's executive arm, said at a briefing in Brussels.\par \par \uc2 But whether they will actually be deployed remains to be seen. For one thing, the prospect of retaliation has made the European corporate world uncomfortable. Many company executives fear that any retaliation could infuriate Mr. Trump and expose them to even higher U.S. tariffs. The luxury, cosmetics and food industries in particular have been lobbying in Brussels against enacting any countermeasures.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Gill declined to say whether the \uc2 European Union\uc2  would still strike back if the United States imposed a lower 15 percent tariff on European imports, similar to the terms of the deal the United States made with Japan.\par \par \uc2 ''Right now, our focus is on finding a negotiated outcome with the U.S.,'' he said, adding that one is ''within reach.''\par \par \uc2 European trade officials have been negotiating furiously with their American counterparts to avoid escalating a trade war after Mr. Trump's threat of a 30 percent tariff blindsided the Europeans, who had been expecting a 10 percent duty.\par \par \uc2 The decision of whether to close a trade deal will ultimately fall on Mr. Trump, who has long been critical of the \uc2 European Union\uc2  for running a trade surplus with the United States.\par \par \uc2 ''The E.U. really, really, really wants to make a deal,'' Howard Lutnick, the commerce secretary, said in an interview on Thursday morning.\par \par \uc2 European exports to the United States reached \u8364\'20\'AC532 billion last year. A 10 percent tax on sales of those goods would reduce European growth by around 0.3 percentage points in the first year, but a 30 percent tax would create a bigger bang, according to an analysis by Oxford Economics.\par \par \uc2 Negotiators in Brussels have been keeping the threat of retaliation on the back burner. But Mr. Trump's on-again, off-again tariff threats galvanized politicians, including President \uc2 Emmanuel Macron\uc2  of France, who said the \uc2 European Union\uc2  had no choice but to present a show of force.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  secretary, Scott Bessent, said Wednesday that Washington was making progress on the negotiations. More talks between the bloc's top trade negotiator and its American counterpart are planned this week.\par \par \uc2 Even so, at a meeting in Berlin on Wednesday night, Mr. Macron and Germany's chancellor, \uc2 Friedrich Merz\uc2 , said they were ready to ''take additional measures'' if the negotiations were not satisfactory.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 European Union\uc2 's retaliation package combines two separate tariff lists. The first would place higher duties on \u8364\'20\'AC21 billion worth of American goods in response to Mr. Trump's steel and aluminum tariffs. The second widened the list of targeted U.S. products to include \uc2 Boeing\uc2  airplanes and Kentucky bourbon.\par \par \uc2 The idea is to deploy the combined package if the Trump administration allows 30 percent tariffs against the bloc to start in August. Mr. Merz warned recently that tariffs that high ''would hit the German export industry to the core.''\par \par \uc2 It is less clear how much backing there will be to deploy a counterattack if the two sides negotiate a 15 percent tariff. One European official, who spoke on the condition of anonymity because the trade talks are private, said retaliation would depend on the final shape of any deal.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, some European companies are pleading for restraint, especially those in industries that symbolize European or American know-how. Earlier this year, for example, when Europe threatened to tax Kentucky bourbon at a higher rate in retaliation for Mr. Trump's initial threat of a 20 percent tariff on imported E.U. goods, the president hit back with a threat to tax Champagne at 200 percent.\par \par \uc2 ''We want negotiations -- companies want to keep the market unblocked instead of pursuing a hard line,'' \uc2 Marc-Antoine Jamet\uc2 , the secretary general of the \uc2 LVMH Mo\u235\'00\'EBt Hennessy-Louis Vuitton\uc2  group, said at a business conference in Paris last week. Should Europe target American goods anew, ''we will become collateral victims, symbols of a combat that is not ours.''\par \par \uc2 Germany's leading automakers, especially \uc2 BMW\uc2  and \uc2 Mercedes-Benz\uc2 , which produce vehicles in the United States for export back to Europe, have lobbied against retaliatory measures, fearing that they would be doubly penalized.\par \par \uc2 Even the European beauty industry is wringing its hands. The United States is the world's leading buyer of European cosmetics. American consumers purchase \u8364\'20\'AC2.8 billion worth of French creams and perfumes alone, according to the French Federation of Beauty Enterprises. Should negotiators in Brussels target American cosmetics imports, the fear is that tariffs on made-in-Europe fragrances and skin care will zoom higher.\par \par \uc2 ''We don't want to be targeted,'' said Emmanuel Guichard, the director general of the federation.\par \par \uc2 Their main concern is getting squeezed between big industrial players, like \uc2 Airbus\uc2  and \uc2 Boeing\uc2 , and European automakers that are much bigger prey for U.S. tariffs. In May, the \uc2 Airbus\uc2  chief executive, \uc2 Guillaume Faury\uc2 , said that if negotiations between Europe and the United States did not result in the cancellation of tariffs on aerospace, Europe should enact retaliatory measures against U.S. aircraft manufacturers.\par \par \uc2 Jeanna Smialek contributed reporting from Brussels, Melissa Eddy from Berlin and Ana Swanson from Washington.\par \par \uc2 Jeanna Smialek contributed reporting from Brussels, Melissa Eddy from Berlin and Ana Swanson from Washington.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250725el7p00015\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
