\par\par \uc2 GUEST ESSAY\par \uc2 Editorial Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Cities and States Can Lead in the Trump Era\b0\par\par\uc2 By Robert Gordon and Jennifer Pahlka \par \uc2 1,086 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 July 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 20\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 President Trump's domestic policy law will create big budget problems for many states and cities. Budget shortfalls aren't new, and neither is the playbook that leaders usually follow. They make across-the-board cuts to programs and raise fees and maybe taxes. Inside government agencies, they emphasize sharing the pain: implementing hiring freezes, instituting furlough days for public employees and laying off workers based on seniority. Nobody is happy, but at least the approach is equitable and minimizes disruption.\par \par \uc2 This is the wrong playbook. Those who follow the across-the-board approach will end up cutting teachers or raising subway fares while protecting jobs that could have been automated years ago. The public is already frustrated with government for unkept promises and often sluggish services. If our leaders simply manage cuts to avoid upheaval, that frustration will only rise. Now is the time for intelligent disruption.\par \par \uc2 Government feels like it's stuck in an earlier era because it often is, operating in a model that hasn't served us for decades. Our industrial-age bureaucracies were slow to adapt to the internet era, and now they risk missing the boat again as the world hurtles into a new technological revolution. As budgets shrink and public needs grow because of this new law, defending the status quo isn't just inefficient; it's also immoral.\par \par \uc2 To transform the operations of the public sector, leaders will need both courage and creativity. Government unions and contractors alike will be uncomfortable. The question is whether to prioritize the needs of the existing system or the needs of the public it is supposed to serve. A few pioneers are choosing the public, responding to the coming crisis by ensuring that our public institutions have the right people doing the right work. Other states and cities should follow their lead.\par \par \uc2 In Denver, which is facing a budget shortfall, the city recently moved to change its layoff rules. In many states and cities, layoffs must be based on seniority, and a more senior employee can bump a more effective junior one. Mayor Mike Johnston changed that, and Denver's new rules instead instruct managers to weigh employees' performance history, abilities and length of service.\par \par \uc2 At the meeting where the city's Career Service Board approved these changes, a crowd that included city workers booed and shouted, ''Shame.'' But Denverites should applaud their mayor for putting the government's ability to serve the public first.\par \par \uc2 Cutting the right work is as important as cutting the right staff members, and David Chiu, the San Francisco city attorney, shows the way. His city's regulations are full of unnecessary signoffs, meetings and reports, which helps explain why building a public toilet there was notoriously slated to cost the city $1.7 million.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Chiu's team got to work with experts from \uc2 Stanford University\uc2  and used an artificial intelligence model to identify 528 separate requirements for detailed staff reports. Many were outdated, such as one addressing sidewalk newspaper racks that no longer exist. Mr. Chiu recommended eliminating just under a third of these requirements. Now the city is expanding the effort to examine the excessive number of boards and commissions. New Jersey is also a model of smart, safe A.I. use: It has trained more than 14,000 employees to use A.I. tools to work more efficiently. Meanwhile, New York passed a law barring A.I. from replacing any part of a state employee's job duties. As budget pressures mount, New York may well need to cut critical services while preserving obsolete positions -- and serve its citizens worse than New Jersey does.\par \par \uc2 Yes, as in the private sector, the smart use of A.I. will disrupt work flows and eliminate some roles. But the goal shouldn't be protecting every public sector job; it should be serving the public better. Smart leaders will retrain displaced workers for roles only humans can fill: solving complex problems, engaging with residents and making nuanced decisions. Government is full of talented, dedicated people, and we should use their talents to the fullest.\par \par \uc2 Another source of waste is outside consultants paid vast sums to operate technology systems that government employees lack the expertise to run. These consultants usually have incentives to prolong projects or design complex solutions that increase costs.\par \par \uc2 A smarter approach is for governments to hire people with the skills to master critical technology systems. Gov. \uc2 Wes Moore\uc2  of Maryland has replaced some contractors with staff members to oversee the state's public benefits system, saving millions and building a more user-friendly system at the same time. Thanks in part to a new digital team, Pennsylvania has cut the time it takes to issue certain licenses from eight weeks to two days.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump's new budget law will make it even more important that states run efficient technology for their Medicaid programs. The law imposes new requirements for many Medicaid recipients to prove they work. When Georgia and Arkansas established work requirements, people often lost or were denied coverage because of bureaucratic errors resulting from confusing and buggy I.T. systems. Strong state teams, using A.I. well, can reduce this unfairness.\par \par \uc2 Changing how government operates won't fix the new law or solve the budget crisis ahead. The federal cuts are simply too deep. But it can do something equally important: prove that government can work better when leaders show political courage.\par \par \uc2 Anti-government cynicism helped create this mess in the first place. The best way to fight back is to deliver results. Leaders who choose reform over preservation may find that their constituents start believing in them again. The budget crisis may be unavoidable, but the loss of public trust doesn't have to be.\par \par \uc2 Robert Gordon is a visiting fellow at \uc2 Georgetown University\uc2  and a former senior official at the White House and the \uc2 Michigan Department of Health and Human Services\uc2 . Jennifer Pahlka is a senior fellow at the Niskanen Center, a former U.S. deputy chief technology officer and the author of ''Recoding America.''\par \par \uc2 The Times is committed to publishing a diversity of letters to the editor. We'd like to hear what you think about this or any of our articles. Here are some tips . And here's our email: letters@nytimes.com .\par \par \uc2 Follow the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  Opinion section on \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , \uc2 Instagram\uc2 , TikTok, Bluesky, \uc2 WhatsApp\uc2  and Threads.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250725el7p00007\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
