\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 In Letter, NASA Workers Object to Proposed Cuts\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kenneth Chang \par \uc2 1,101 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 July 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 11\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The signatories of the ''Voyager Declaration'' warned the space agency's leadership about the consequences of major budget cuts that would halt many science missions.\par \par \uc2 A public letter from \uc2 NASA\uc2  employees on Monday urges leaders of the space agency not to carry out deep cuts sought by the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 ''We are compelled to speak up when our leadership prioritizes political momentum over human safety, scientific advancement and efficient use of public resources,'' the employees wrote in the letter. It is addressed to \uc2 Sean Duffy\uc2 , the secretary of transportation, whom President Trump appointed this month as acting \uc2 NASA\uc2  administrator.\par \par \uc2 Cuts to \uc2 NASA\uc2  programs have been arbitrary and in defiance of priorities set by Congress, the \uc2 NASA\uc2  employees said. ''The consequences for the agency and the country alike are dire,'' they wrote.\par \par \uc2 In an email statement, Bethany Stevens, the \uc2 NASA\uc2  press secretary, said \uc2 NASA\uc2  would never compromise on safety. ''Any reductions -- including our current voluntary reduction -- will be designed to protect safety-critical roles,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 ''To ensure \uc2 NASA\uc2  delivers for the American people, we are continually evaluating mission lifecycles, not on sustaining outdated or lower-priority missions,'' Ms. Stevens said.\par \par \uc2 Upheaval within \uc2 NASA\uc2  continued on Monday, when Makenzie Lystrup, director of \uc2 NASA\uc2 's \uc2 Goddard Space Flight Center\uc2  in Greenbelt, Md., announced she was ''stepping aside'' on Aug. 1. In an email sent to center employees at 12:45 p.m. and shared with The Times, she did not give a reason for her departure. Cynthia Simmons, the center's deputy director, will take over as acting director.\par \par \uc2 ''I am honored to have been part of this incredible journey with you,'' Dr. Lystrup wrote.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 NASA\uc2  letter follows similar letters of criticism by federal employees at the \uc2 National Institutes of Health\uc2  and the \uc2 Environmental Protection Agency\uc2 . Dr. Jay Bhattacharya, the N.I.H. director, said he welcomed respectful dissent, but the E.P.A. placed 144 employees who signed that agency's letter on leave.\par \par \uc2 . ''We're scared of retaliation,'' said Monica Gorman, an operations research analyst at the Goddard. She said that staff ''go to the bathroom to talk to each other, and look under the stalls to make sure that no one else is there before we talk.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Gorman is one of 287 current and former \uc2 NASA\uc2  employees who signed the letter, although more than half did so anonymously. More than 15,000 people work at the space agency. Prominent scientists outside of \uc2 NASA\uc2 , including 20 Nobel Prize winners, also offered their names in support.\par \par \uc2 The N.I.H. letter inspired some people at \uc2 NASA\uc2  to put together a similar effort. The N.I.H. letter writers had called their dissent the ''Bethesda Declaration'' -- the agency is in Bethesda, Md. -- and the \uc2 NASA\uc2  letter writers called theirs the ''Voyager Declaration,'' in honor of Voyager 1 and Voyager 2, the two \uc2 NASA\uc2  space probes launched in the 1970s that continue to function as they fly through interstellar space.\par \par \uc2 ''One of the messages that \uc2 NASA\uc2  management has been passing down to every employee is that no one is coming to save us, including Congress,'' said one of the organizers of the letter, who spoke on the condition of anonymity out of fear for retaliation. ''So the Voyager Declaration is one effort to raise our voices and speak out to save ourselves,'' the person added.\par \par \uc2 The letter is framed as a ''formal dissent'' -- an official process at \uc2 NASA\uc2  for registering disagreements that managers may not want to hear. It was part of changes instituted at \uc2 NASA\uc2  after the losses of the Columbia and Challenger space shuttles when concerns of some engineers were brushed aside.\par \par \uc2 Stand Up for Science, a nonprofit organization that has organized opposition to the Trump administration's cutbacks of science research, has helped coordinate the letters to the three agencies.\par \par \uc2 In its budget request to Congress in June, the Trump administration proposed slashing \uc2 NASA\uc2 's budget by almost 25 percent. \uc2 NASA\uc2 's science mission directorate, which includes earth science, solar system missions and astrophysics, would face a cut of 47 percent, to $3.9 billion from $7.3 billion. Nineteen currently operating science missions, including the Chandra X-ray Observatory, the Juno mission at Jupiter and the two Orbiting Carbon Observatories, would be turned off and discarded.\par \par \uc2 In her statement, Ms. Stevens, the \uc2 NASA\uc2  press secretary, said, ''The reality is that President Trump has proposed billions of dollars for \uc2 NASA\uc2  science, demonstrating an ongoing commitment to communicating our scientific achievements.''\par \par \uc2 Congress does not appear to agree with making draconian cuts. A Senate subcommittee proposed providing $7.3 billion for \uc2 NASA\uc2 's science mission directorate for fiscal year 2026, the same amount as the current year. Its counterpart in the House of Representatives was less generous, proposing $6 billion for the science missions.\par \par \uc2 While \uc2 NASA\uc2  has so far avoided widespread layoffs, thousands of employees have left or are planning to leave under early retirement and buyout offers.\par \par \uc2 ''Some I know have left because they want to make space for other younger people to stay,'' Ms. Gorman said.\par \par \uc2 One complaint of the letter writers is that \uc2 NASA\uc2  leaders have made decisions based on the president's proposed cuts, without waiting to see what Congress will provide.\par \par \uc2 Another concern is that even if Congress provides money for science missions, the administration could refuse to spend it.\par \par \uc2 Last week, in a letter to Mr. Duffy, two Democratic members of the House Committee on Science, Space and Technology -- \uc2 Zoe Lofgren\uc2  of California and Valerie Foushee of North Carolina -- accused \uc2 NASA\uc2  of illegally implementing Mr. Trump's priorities.\par \par \uc2 ''Congress directs \uc2 NASA\uc2  to spend money on certain missions, programs and priorities; and Congress sets the policies that \uc2 NASA\uc2  must implement,'' Ms. Lofgren and Ms. Foushee wrote. ''These congressional actions are not friendly requests. They are the law.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Gorman said her department at the Goddard Research Center had been informed that it would not be financed next year.\par \par \uc2 ''Things are already not OK right now, and once senior scientists and senior engineers and people leave, that's it,'' she said. ''That knowledge is gone, and we're all going to suffer from that brain drain for years and years.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Gorman, who is a lead organizer for a union that represents 1,700 scientists, engineers and technicians at Goddard, has been outspoken.\par \par \uc2 ''I'm already at risk of losing my job, and I'd rather speak out and try to save something at \uc2 NASA\uc2 , rather than just hide under my desk until I get laid off,'' she said. ''But I am scared.''\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250722el7m0003z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
