\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 The Digest\b0\par\par\uc2 By The Associated Press \par \uc2 521 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 July 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 LABOR\par \par \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  Aims to Curb Corporate Remote Work\par \par \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  is requiring some remote workers to return to its headquarters and increasing the number of days that employees are required to work there.\par \par \uc2 In a letter to employees posted on Monday, the company's chairman and chief executive, \uc2 Brian Niccol\uc2 , said corporate employees would need to be in the office four days a week starting in early October instead of three.\par \par \uc2 The Seattle-based company said that all corporate ''people leaders'' must be based in either Seattle or Toronto within 12 months. \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  said individual employees working under those leaders would not be asked to relocate, but the company said all hiring for future roles and lateral moves will require employees to be based in Seattle or Toronto. Mr. Niccol said affected workers who choose not to relocate will be eligible for a one-time voluntary exit program with a cash payment.\par \par \uc2 While many workers grew to enjoy working from home during the pandemic, the call for workers to return to offices has been growing. Major employers such as \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , \uc2 AT&T\uc2  and the federal government have required employees to work in company sites five days per week. Competition for fully remote jobs is fierce.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Niccol was not required to relocate to Seattle when he was hired to lead \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  last August. Instead, the company said it would help him set up an office near his home in Newport Beach, Calif., and would give him the use of a corporate jet to commute to Seattle. Associated Press\par \par \uc2 COMPANIES\par \par \uc2 Kenvue\uc2 's Chief Leaves Consumer Health Division\par \par \uc2 Kenvue\uc2 , the maker of Listerine and Band-Aid brands, says that Thibaut Mongon is leaving as chief executive as the company continues with a strategic internal review.\par \par \uc2 Kenvue\uc2  used to be a part of \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2 , which announced in late 2021 that it was splitting its consumer health division from the pharmaceutical and medical device divisions in a bid to make each more nimble.\par \par \uc2 Kenvue\uc2  said Monday that a board member, \uc2 Kirk Perry\uc2 , will serve as interim chief executive, effective immediately.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Perry has served as a director since December and most recently served as chief executive of \uc2 Circana\uc2 , which provides market research. He spent more than 20 years at \uc2 Procter & Gamble\uc2  in various roles.\par \par \uc2 Kenvue\uc2  also said that it is continuing to work with its strategic review committee to explore options for the company.\par \par \uc2 ''The Board's strategic review is underway, and we are considering a broad range of potential alternatives, including ways to simplify the Company's portfolio and how it operates,'' the board's chair, \uc2 Larry Merlo\uc2 , said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 Kenvue\uc2  provided some preliminary results for its fiscal second quarter.\par \par \uc2 The company anticipates adjusted earnings in a range of 28 cents to 29 cents per share.\par \par \uc2 Analysts polled by \uc2 FactSet\uc2  expect earnings of 28 cents per share.\par \par \uc2 Kenvue\uc2  will report its full quarterly results on Aug. 7 and said that it will revise its full-year outlook on that date.\par \par \uc2 Shares rose more than 4 percent before the market open. Associated Press\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250715el7f0004m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
