\par\par \uc2 The Arts/Cultural Desk; SECTC\par \b \uc2 Video Game Actors End Contract Dispute Over A.I.\b0\par\par\uc2 By Reggie Ugwu \par \uc2 509 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 July 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 5\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The actors went on an 11-month strike against the studios behind Call of Duty and other games because of concerns that visual and voice replicas would reduce their work.\par \par \uc2 After striking for nearly 11 months, actors approved a new contract with major video game studios on Wednesday night that addressed concerns about the use of artificial intelligence to create digital replicas.\par \par \uc2 The agreement with \uc2 SAG-AFTRA\uc2  requires the companies behind some of the world's most popular games, including Call of Duty, Mortal Kombat and Apex Legends, to obtain the written consent of performers to create visual or vocal replicas. Studios must also pay performers for the use of replicas at a rate comparable to in-person work.\par \par \uc2 ''I feel incredibly relieved,'' said Jennifer Hale, a voice actor known for her work on Mass Effect, Overwatch and other games. ''I feel more confident that the work I do will not be abused or misused.''\par \par \uc2 The deal, which was approved by more than 95 percent of voters, echoes a framework established during the union's strike against film and television studios two summers ago.\par \par \uc2 ''Members can go to work and feel confident that the work they're doing today isn't going to harm their career tomorrow,'' Duncan Crabtree-Ireland, the national executive director and chief negotiator of \uc2 SAG-AFTRA\uc2 , said in an interview.\par \par \uc2 Video games have long relied on human actors to perform dialogue or model the appearance of digital characters. Actors were worried that powerful generative A.I. software could be used to replicate or repurpose their work indefinitely without their involvement.\par \par \uc2 In May, Fortnite unveiled an A.I.-powered Darth Vader who responds to players in the booming voice of James Earl Jones; before the actor died last year, he had agreed to let A.I. learn from his vocal performances.\par \par \uc2 In addition to the A.I. guardrails, the union's new contract includes annual pay raises for members through 2027, as well as new safety measures, such as a requirement that a medic be present for hazardous action performances.\par \par \uc2 The actors ended their strike last month after a tentative deal had been reached. A future strike would automatically suspend their consent for the use of digital replicas to generate new material.\par \par \uc2 The bargaining group for video game studios included \uc2 Activision\uc2 , \uc2 Electronic Arts\uc2 , Warner Bros. Games and six other companies. Audrey Cooling, a spokeswoman for the group, said in a statement that it was pleased the deal delivered ''historic wage increases'' and ''industry-leading A.I. protections.''\par \par \uc2 ''We look forward to building on our industry's decades-long partnership with the union and continuing to create groundbreaking entertainment experiences for billions of players worldwide,'' the statement added.\par \par \uc2 The actors went on strike last July after a lengthy effort to renegotiate a previous agreement that had expired in November 2022. The pay raises and safety measures had been agreed to by that point, leaving artificial intelligence as the sole dispute.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250712el7c0003f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
