\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECT\par \b \uc2 Dozens Rescued From Tunnel That Collapsed in Los Angeles\b0\par\par\uc2 By Mimi Dwyer and John Yoon \par \uc2 467 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 July 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 The New York Times on the Web\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The workers climbed over a mound of soil nearly 15 feet tall inside the tunnel, before being shuttled to the entry point more than five miles away.\par \par \uc2 Twenty-seven workers inside a collapsed tunnel in Los Angeles climbed over a mound of soil up to 15 feet tall and emerged at the only entrance five miles away without major injury, making an improbable escape on Wednesday night, officials said.\par \par \uc2 Four other tunnel workers went inside the industrial tunnel after the collapse to help in the rescue efforts. All 31 workers emerged safely after the collapse, said Michael Chee, the spokesman for the Los Angeles County Sanitation Districts.\par \par \uc2 The Los Angeles Fire Department said that no one was missing after it had dispatched more than 100 rescue workers to the site in the city's Wilmington neighborhood, about 20 miles south of downtown Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 ''Tonight, we were lucky,'' interim Fire Department Chief Ronnie Villanueva said at a news conference.\par \par \uc2 The collapse left a pile of loose soil that was 12 to 15 feet tall inside a horizontal excavation site in the tunnel, which was part of a construction project, the Fire Department said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 The tunnel workers who had been trapped by the debris scrambled over it and met their co-workers who had gone inside to help. The workers then shuttled on a tunnel vehicle several at a time on a tunnel vehicle, fire officials said.\par \par \uc2 Television footage from local news broadcasters showed the workers being raised to the ground from the tunnel's circular entrance inside a metal cage that was hoisted by a crane.\par \par \uc2 The authorities did not immediately say what had caused the collapse. Interim Chief Villanueva said that the workers were being treated for minor injuries.\par \par \uc2 The tunnel was part of a $630 million wastewater management project commissioned by the Sanitation Districts. FlatironDragados, the main contractor of the tunnel, which was seven miles long and 18 feet wide, said on its website that the project was expected to be completed in 2027.\par \par \uc2 Construction workers were building a new tunnel to transport treated wastewater to the ocean, replacing two tunnels that had been in operation since the 1930s, Mr. Chee said.\par \par \uc2 Robert Ferrante, the chief engineer and general manager of the Sanitation Districts, said that construction would be paused indefinitely while the cause of the collapse was being determined. He said that the collapse had occurred in a section where the ground exerted high pressure on the tunnel while workers were operating a boring machine elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 Mayor \uc2 Karen Bass\uc2  told reporters outside the facility that she had rushed to the site fearing tragedy.\par \par \uc2 ''We're all blessed tonight in Los Angeles,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250710el7a0005g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
