\par\par \uc2 STOCKS &AMP; BONDS\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 S&P 500 Hits High as Data Shows a Resilient Job Market\b0\par\par\uc2 By The Associated Press \par \uc2 672 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 July 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 U.S. stocks climbed further into record heights on Thursday after a report showed the U.S. job market looks stronger than Wall Street expected.\par \par \uc2 The S&P 500 rose 0.8 percent and set a high for the fourth time in five days. The Dow Jones industrial average added 344 points, or 0.8 percent, and the Nasdaq composite gained 1 percent.\par \par \uc2 The market's gains were widespread, and companies whose profits can get the biggest boosts when workers are feeling confident helped lead the way. \uc2 Expedia\uc2  climbed 3.2 percent, and \uc2 Norwegian Cruise Line\uc2  steamed 2.9 percent higher.\par \par \uc2 Bank stocks were also strong, with \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  up 2.3 percent, and \uc2 JPMorgan Chase\uc2  up 1.9 percent.\par \par \uc2 The reaction was bigger in the bond market after the report from the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2 , which said employers added 147,000 more jobs to their payrolls last month than they cut. The unexpected acceleration in hiring signals the job market is holding up despite worries about how President Trump's tariffs may hurt the economy and inflation.\par \par \uc2 ''There is nothing to complain about here,'' according to \uc2 Carl Weinberg\uc2 , chief economist at \uc2 High Frequency Economics\uc2 . ''You cannot find any evidence of a nascent recession in these figures.''\par \par \uc2 A separate report, meanwhile, said fewer U.S. workers applied for unemployment benefits last week, indicating easing layoffs.\par \par \uc2 Yields jumped in the bond market as investors bet the better-than-expected data could keep the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  on hold when it comes to interest rates, instead of cutting them like Mr. Trump has loudly been calling for.\par \par \uc2 Traders in the futures market now see less than a 5 percent chance that the Fed could cut its main interest rate at its meeting this month. That's down sharply from the nearly 24 percent chance they saw just a day earlier, according to data from \uc2 CME Group\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Fed's chair, \uc2 Jerome H. Powell\uc2 , has been insisting that he wants to wait and see how Mr. Trump's tariffs affect the economy and inflation before making its next move. While lower rates stimulate the economy by making it easier to borrow money, they can also give inflation more fuel. And that could be dangerous if Mr. Trump's tariffs are about to send inflation higher.\par \par \uc2 Many of Mr. Trump's stiff proposed taxes on imports are currently paused, but they're scheduled to kick in next week unless Mr. Trump reaches deals with other countries to lower them.\par \par \uc2 Many U.S. companies in the services industries are still saying they're concerned about the impacts of tariffs, even if they returned to growth last month after May's contraction, according to the most recent survey by the \uc2 Institute for Supply Management\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''Increased cost from tariffs and the potential for tariffs is impacting cost increases,'' one company in the agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting industry said in the survey.\par \par \uc2 The yield on the 10-year Treasury rose to 4.35 percent from 4.30 percent late Wednesday. The two-year Treasury yield, which moves more closely with expectations for the Fed, jumped even more. It climbed to 3.88 percent from 3.78 percent.\par \par \uc2 On Wall Street, \uc2 Datadog\uc2  rallied 14.9 percent after learning that its stock will join the widely followed S&P 500 index before trading begins on Wednesday. Many managers of funds either directly mimic or at least compare themselves against the S&P 500, which drives investment into any stock that joins the index.\par \par \uc2 Datadog\uc2  will replace \uc2 Juniper Networks\uc2 , which combined with \uc2 Hewlett Packard Enterprise\uc2  in a merger.\par \par \uc2 On the losing side of Wall Street were companies that can feel pain from interest rates staying high. Homebuilders, for example, would like rates to fall to make mortgages cheaper, and \uc2 Lennar\uc2  sank 4.1 percent, while \uc2 D.R. Horton\uc2  dropped 2.7 percent.\par \par \uc2 In stock markets abroad, indexes rose across much of Europe and Asia.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250704el740004b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
