\par\par \uc2 The Arts/Cultural Desk; SECT\par \b \uc2 Combs Avoids Life in Prison In Split Verdict\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ben Sisario and Julia Jacobs \par \uc2 513 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 July 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 The New York Times on the Web\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Sean Combs, the hip-hop mogul who built a business empire around his personal brand, was convicted on Wednesday of transporting prostitutes to participate in his drug-fueled sex marathons, but acquitted of racketeering and sex trafficking, the most serious charges against him. A judge denied him bail and ordered Mr. Combs to remain in detention until sentencing, which is still unscheduled.\par \par \uc2 Though Mr. Combs, 55, still faces a potential sentence of as much as 20 years in prison, he and his lawyers were jubilant after the acquittals on the more severe charges in an indictment that accused the famed producer of coercing women into unwanted sex with male prostitutes, aided by a team of pliant employees.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Combs had faced a possible life sentence. Under the transportation charges, set by the federal Mann Act, each of the two convictions carries a maximum term of 10 years, but the judge could order the sentences to be served concurrently.\par \par \uc2 Here's what else to know:\par \par \uc2 Combs's reaction: After the verdict was read, Mr. Combs put his hands together and mouthed ''thank you, thank you'' at the jurors. Later, he dropped to his knees, apparently in prayer, and started a round of applause in court. His supporters and family began clapping and whistling for his legal team, who embraced one another. Read more >\par \par \uc2 Bail denied: The defense immediately argued for Mr. Combs's release, arguing that jurors ''resoundingly rejected the government's depiction of Mr. Combs.'' The judge declined to release him, noting that at trial, ''the defense conceded'' his violence in his personal relationships.\par \par \uc2 The jury: The racially diverse panel of eight men and four women, ranging in age from 30 to 74, sent several notes to the judge during deliberations, both asking for evidence and expressing concern about one juror. Read more >\par \par \uc2 Defense team celebrates: On the courthouse steps, Marc Agnifilo, the lead attorney, said: ''It's a great victory for Sean Combs. It's a great victory for the jury system.'' He added that the jury had acquitted Mr. Combs of racketeering conspiracy and sex-trafficking, crimes he was ''absolutely innocent of.''\par \par \uc2 Women respond: Douglas Wigdor, a lawyer for Casandra Ventura, said of Mr. Combs: ''He's finally been held responsible for two federal crimes, something that he's never faced in his life.'' Arisha Hatch, the interim executive director of the women's advocacy group UltraViolet, called the verdict ''an indictment of a culture in which not believing women and victims of sexual assault remains endemic.'' Read more >\par \par \uc2 Prosecution's statement: Jay Clayton, interim U.S. Attorney for the Southern District of New York, which prosecuted the case, said in a statement: ''Sex crimes deeply scar victims, and the disturbing reality is that sex crimes are all too present in many aspects of our society. Victims endure gut-wrenching physical and mental abuse, leading to lasting trauma. New Yorkers and all Americans want this scourge stopped and perpetrators brought to justice.''\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250703el730003v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
