\par\par \uc2 CALCULATOR\par \uc2 Real Estate Desk; SECTRE\par \b \uc2 More Demand Than Supply\b0\par\par\uc2 By Arnesa A. Howell \par \uc2 396 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 June 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 For teachers, nurses and other skilled workers, few options.\par \par \uc2 In an ideal housing market, there would be a home that is affordable for every household based on income, from the lowest earners all the way to the wealthiest buyers. That kind of balance is hard to achieve, but as a new report reveals, middle-income households are being squeezed out of the market, even as listing inventory rebounds from its post-pandemic lows.\par \par \uc2 According to the 2025 Housing Affordability & Supply report from the National Association of Realtors and Realtor.com, middle-income earners, who make up the largest share of the nation's households, have seen the greatest improvement in housing supply among all income groups. And yet, they can afford only 21.2 percent of all available listings. That's up from 20.8 percent a year ago -- but a far cry from the 49 percent they could afford in 2019.\par \par \uc2 The report, which sorted households by income level to examine the balance of purchasing power, defined ''middle-income'' households as those earning $75,000 annually, and showed that this demographic faced the largest housing shortage among all groups.\par \par \uc2 Ideally, explained Nadia Evangelou, senior economist and director of real estate research at the \uc2 National Association of Realtors\uc2 , households earning $75,000 a year should still be able to afford around half of all available listings across the country. But for that to happen, the market would need to add another 416,000 listings priced at or below $255,000, the ceiling of affordability for typical middle-income earners such as teachers, nurses and other skilled workers.\par \par \uc2 Households making $100,000 a year have also seen a huge shortfall since 2019 in the number of listings they can afford, according to the report, while lower-income households, which represent a third of all U.S. households, ''have almost no access to for-sale homes.''\par \par \uc2 In the middle, buyers are losing ground because building a home for under $260,000 is increasingly difficult -- the result of rising land costs, zoning rules and the price of materials, according to Ms. Evangelou.\par \par \uc2 ''The middle-income paradox is that we have the largest gains for this income group in affordable housing supply but still continue to have the largest gaps in affordability and availability of homes,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250629el6t00045\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
