\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Democrats in Congress Implore Rubio to Reject Mass State Dept. Layoffs\b0\par\par\uc2 By Edward Wong and Michael Crowley \par \uc2 920 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 June 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 17\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Democrats say they are worried about reports that about 700 career diplomats will be fired. The agency has said it plans to lay off 2,000 employees soon.\par \par \uc2 Sixty Democratic lawmakers told Secretary of State \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  on Friday to refrain from moving ahead with mass layoffs of State Department employees and to lift a hiring freeze at a time of widening global crises.\par \par \uc2 In a letter to Mr. Rubio, the lawmakers said they were concerned about reported plans to fire about 700 career diplomats, known as Foreign Service officers, based mainly on the fact that those employees are currently posted in the United States rather than overseas.\par \par \uc2 Diplomats spend much of their career abroad but rotate through Washington and other U.S. posts such as the United Nations, so firing people who happen to be currently on assignment in the country is seen as arbitrary and unfair by many State Department employees, as well as by the lawmakers.\par \par \uc2 ''Hiring and assignment freezes as well as mass firings of these diplomatic experts leave the U.S. with limited tools to engage as a leader on the world stage during this critical juncture,'' the lawmakers wrote in the letter, which Representative \uc2 Donald S. Beyer Jr.\uc2 , Democrat of Virginia, helped to organize.\par \par \uc2 Information indicating that diplomats could ''be penalized for their present duty domestic stations is especially concerning, and not conducive to a thoughtful, priorities-driven reorganization process that retains the best talent and recognizes the unique nature of the Foreign Service.''\par \par \uc2 The lawmakers also said they disapproved of Mr. Rubio's freezing the administering of the Foreign Service officer test, which had been done three times a year and has been critical for hiring new diplomats. The lawmakers asked him to reinstate the test.\par \par \uc2 In April, Mr. Rubio released what he called a reorganization plan, which includes cutting more than 100 offices -- including those focusing on global criminal justice, women's issues, and democracy and human rights -- and firing hundreds of employees working in the United States.\par \par \uc2 In a statement at the time, Mr. Rubio said the plan would address a ''bloated, bureaucratic'' department ''beholden to radical political ideology.'' His department later sent a memo to Congress with more details, including plans to fire about 2,000 people. The State Department says it aims to do the overhaul by July 1.\par \par \uc2 A U.S. official said about 1,300 of those expected to be fired are Civil Service employees, who spend their career mainly in the United States, and 700 are career diplomats, who rotate among overseas and domestic posts on tours.\par \par \uc2 A federal judge in California on June 13 ordered the State Department to pause the planned firings.\par \par \uc2 The ruling froze the departure of about 40 Civil Service employees who worked in a State Department office on countering foreign disinformation that Mr. Rubio and Darren Beattie, a political appointee, had ordered to be shut down. Eight career diplomats in that office were being moved elsewhere in the department.\par \par \uc2 The decision to shut down that office drew criticism from some congressional officials, including aides of Senate Republicans.\par \par \uc2 The Trump administration has asked the Supreme Court to block the judge's orders on the agencies. The justices are expected to rule on the request soon, as tens of thousands of federal employees anxiously await word about layoffs.\par \par \uc2 The union that represents Foreign Service officers issued a statement on Wednesday demanding that the State Department observe the court order and hold off on any layoffs.\par \par \uc2 ''Sources inside the department tell us that layoffs will be announced as soon as the end of this week or early next week,'' Tom Yazdgerdi, the president of the \uc2 American Foreign Service Association\uc2 , said in the statement. ''Unless the Supreme Court intervenes, the department is legally barred from taking any action outlined in its reorganization plans.''\par \par \uc2 ''What we are hearing about the planned layoffs would constitute a serious breach of the rule of law,'' Mr. Yazdgerdi added. ''This not only threatens the integrity of the Foreign Service but also undermines the effectiveness of U.S. diplomacy.''\par \par \uc2 The State Department said on Thursday that it ''has no plans to violate a court order'' when asked about the union's statement and the possibility of imminent mass firings.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, the department made changes to a section on ''reduction in force'' policies in the Foreign Affairs Manual, which has terms of employment for career diplomats. That move ignited anxiety among thousands of employees. The changes make it easier for the department to fire diplomats posted in the United States and without taking into account merit, employees said.\par \par \uc2 The State Department released an internal memo on Thursday that said the changes to the manual would allow it to make ''Foreign Service reductions'' among ''specific offices which are duplicative, noncore or where organic efficiencies can be found.'' \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  obtained a copy of the memo.\par \par \uc2 To many officials in the Trump administration, the State Department is an arm of what they see as a ''deep state'' bureaucracy intent on thwarting President Trump's agenda.\par \par \uc2 In February, Mr. Trump issued an executive order titled ''One Voice for America's Foreign Relations'' directing the secretary of state to ''implement reforms in recruiting, performance, evaluation and retention standards'' to ''ensure a work force that is committed to faithful implementation of the president's foreign policy.''\par \par \uc2 Eileen Sullivan contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Eileen Sullivan contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250628el6s00047\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
