\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Trump Anxiety Casts Pall Over Public Service Awards\b0\par\par\uc2 By Elizabeth Williamson \par \uc2 1,088 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 June 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 14\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The Trump administration's large cuts to the federal work force turned an annual celebration of federal workers into a reminder of loss.\par \par \uc2 Every year in Washington, hundreds of federal workers put on gowns and tuxedos to honor colleagues who battle disease, pursue criminals and invent new technology, in what is billed as the Oscars of public service. Tearful honorees call co-workers and families onstage, and cabinet secretaries and the president offer thanks in person or by video.\par \par \uc2 Things looked different this year.\par \par \uc2 These are difficult times to be a nonpartisan federal expert, as the Trump administration has cast civil servants as villains and forced out a quarter-million of them. For the first time in the two-decade history of the Samuel J. Heyman Service to America Medals, the federal employee of the year -- the biggest honor -- was no longer a federal employee.\par \par \uc2 David Lebryk, a former top Treasury Department official, was forced out of his career position for refusing to grant \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  and his \uc2 Department of Government Efficiency\uc2  what he considered unlawful access to the government's payment system.\par \par \uc2 In accepting his award on Tuesday night, Mr. Lebryk noted that ''most of my career was spent trying to be unnoticed.'' But he referred to the circumstances that led to his resignation, and offered a credo for public service.\par \par \uc2 ''It is important to exercise principled leadership, make difficult decisions, have the courage and conviction to stand behind those decisions and be accountable and ultimately prepared to accept the consequences of those decisions,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 There were no other acceptance speeches for awards given at the event -- a departure from previous years -- because some honorees said they were fearful of even inadvertently irking the administration. At least one winner turned down the award because the worker's boss, a Trump appointee, forbade the worker to accept it.\par \par \uc2 Consequently, this year's event also served as ''a reminder of what we're losing,'' said \uc2 Max Stier\uc2 , chief executive of the \uc2 Partnership for Public Service\uc2 , a nonprofit group that promotes an effective federal work force and sponsors the awards each year.\par \par \uc2 The ceremony was moved this year from the John F. Kennedy Center for the Performing Arts, the Washington epicenter of the Trump administration's culture wars, to the new Johns Hopkins University Bloomberg Center, which offered the space for free.\par \par \uc2 Past ceremonies have included such attractions as a panel featuring \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2 . This year a bluegrass band composed of \uc2 National Institutes of Health\uc2  employees, including a sickle cell anemia researcher on bass fiddle, provided entertainment.\par \par \uc2 The Sammies, as they are called, are named for Mr. Heyman, a former assistant U.S. attorney in Connecticut and a financier who founded the \uc2 Partnership for Public Service\uc2  in 2001. This year, 23 winners were chosen from among 350 nominees from 65 agencies.\par \par \uc2 Among the honorees was Maya Bretzius, a strategic adviser at the \uc2 Internal Revenue Service\uc2  who worked to overhaul its call center operations to provide faster help for taxpayers trying to reach roughly 25,000 customer service representatives.\par \par \uc2 In an interview ahead of the ceremony, she said she was committed to completing her project in time for the upcoming tax season, despite losing hundreds of operators to buyouts and resignations.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Bretzius, 35, said she accepted a 50 percent pay cut in 2023 when she joined the I.R.S. from the \uc2 Boston Consulting Group\uc2 . Government ''was a place that aligned with the type of impact I wanted to make in the world,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 ''I wanted people I grew up with in my community who don't always have ways to advocate for themselves to have a government that works efficiently and effectively,'' she added. ''That is a really important part of democracy.''\par \par \uc2 Alexander Maranghides, a fire protection engineer at the \uc2 National Institute of Standards and Technology\uc2 , was honored for decades of research into wildfires that spread to adjacent communities. His team recently condensed thousands of pages of research into a 169-page guide to planning evacuation routes, and an upcoming guide on hardening entire communities against fire.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Maranghides, an immigrant from Greece who helped with the investigation into the Sept. 11 attacks, said that the benefit of government research ''is our neutrality.''\par \par \uc2 ''It's critical to what we do,'' he said. ''We don't own fire engines. We don't own land. We're not responsible for writing building codes. All we do is science.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Lebryk was introduced by Timothy F. Geithner and \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2 , two of the 11 former Treasury secretaries he served.\par \par \uc2 He oversaw the Bureau of the Fiscal Service, which collects government revenue and disburses $6 trillion a year in government payments on behalf of government agencies. He was honored for carrying out a law that improved transparency in government tax collection and spending; for delivering 480 million stimulus payments during the pandemic; and for his use of a fraud detection program that last year clawed back $7 billion in improper payments.\par \par \uc2 In an interview before the ceremony, Mr. Lebryk said he was surprised that his departure had generated such attention for ''a career guy who does this sleepy stuff.'' He said he had ''slept well'' after he was forced to retire, ''because we have the finest people in the fiscal service running these things.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Lebryk grew up in a single-parent household in Indiana, and attended college with the help of federal education grants. ''The federal government can't and shouldn't do everything, but it made a significant difference in my life,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 The night before the awards, he addressed a group of incoming federal interns, encouraging them to pursue public service.\par \par \uc2 Eventually, he said, ''things will break,'' and the administration ''will have to turn to people who know how to fix things.'' He said he tells government colleagues to ''take care of yourself, and take the long view; your skills are going to be needed in the future.''\par \par \uc2 In preparation for Tuesday's program, he dug through photos documenting his 35 years in government. ''I've been in Fort Knox, and sat on the gold. I helped Albania set up a ministry of finance after communism. I walked with George and Barbara Bush in the halls of Versailles,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 ''There is no career I ever could have had that would have given me these experiences and these opportunities,'' he added. ''I feel so lucky.''\par \par \uc2 Eileen Sullivan contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Eileen Sullivan contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250619el6j0003f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
