\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Leaders of 'Orgasmic Meditation' Group Are Convicted\b0\par\par\uc2 By Santul Nerkar \par \uc2 1,359 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 June 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 16\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Nicole Daedone and Rachel Cherwitz were charged with engaging in a forced labor conspiracy related to the sexual wellness company OneTaste.\par \par \uc2 Two women whose company promoted ''orgasmic meditation'' as a tool of female empowerment were found guilty on Monday of running a forced labor scheme in which they coerced vulnerable employees to perform degrading sex acts for little or no pay.\par \par \uc2 The convictions of the women, Nicole Daedone and Rachel Cherwitz, followed a five-week trial in which jurors heard testimony about sex acts rarely described in a courtroom.\par \par \uc2 As the verdict was read, Ms. Daedone, wearing a light blue pantsuit, smiled and nodded, before embracing her lawyer, Jennifer Bonjean. Ms. Cherwitz, dressed in a black blazer, did not visibly react.\par \par \uc2 In the courtroom, Ms. Daedone was emotional as she addressed and embraced her supporters, many of whom continue to be connected to the company, OneTaste. About two dozen supporters packed the courtroom on Monday, as many have since the trial began in early May.\par \par \uc2 Joseph Nocella Jr., the interim U.S. attorney for the Eastern District of New York, said in a statement that the verdict had ''unmasked'' Ms. Daedone and Ms. Cherwitz as ''grifters who preyed on vulnerable victims.''\par \par \uc2 Both women are set to be sentenced in late September. Each faces up to 20 years in prison.\par \par \uc2 At a news conference outside the courthouse after the verdict, Ms. Bonjean said the defense planned to appeal.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Daedone, joining her outside the courthouse, said: ''There is nothing but the spiritual aim that I set out for, and that's the liberation of all people, and the liberation of women. I'll do that wherever I am.''\par \par \uc2 The verdict suggested that jurors had embraced an expansive definition of ''coercion.'' Ms. Daedone and Ms. Cherwitz were not accused of blackmail or threats of violence or the loss of property, coercive acts that have defined previous prosecutions of leaders of sex cults and forced labor schemes.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Daedone started OneTaste in San Francisco in 2004. Her goal, she has said, was to address what she called the gap in sexual satisfaction between men and women. A former prostitute who has described growing up in an abusive household, Ms. Daedone says her teachings were inspired by traditional Buddhist practices.\par \par \uc2 OneTaste gained attention for its central practice, orgasmic meditation, or ''OMing.'' The act typically involved a gloved man systematically stroking the genitals of a woman as she lay in a butterfly position on an array of pillows for 15 minutes. Variants included ''male OMing,'' in which a woman stroked a man's genitals for 15 minutes.\par \par \uc2 The company quickly became popular, with positive media coverage helping to fuel a membership surge. At its height, OneTaste had locations in Austin, Texas; Chicago; Denver; New York; and other cities. In New York, there were OneTaste events in neighborhoods like Hell's Kitchen and the West Village.\par \par \uc2 But as OneTaste grew in size and influence, accusations that the company was fostering an abusive work environment began to emerge. A 2018 article by Bloomberg Businessweek magazine was among the first signs that something might be amiss within the group.\par \par \uc2 The article contained accounts from former OneTaste employees who said they had run up substantial credit card debt to pay for courses and had worked for free while trying to pay off their debts. Several months after the article's publication, the company closed its U.S. locations but continued to operate in London for a short time.\par \par \uc2 In 2023, federal prosecutors in Brooklyn charged Ms. Daedone and Ms. Cherwitz, who joined OneTaste in 2007 and rose quickly to become head of sales, with one count of forced labor conspiracy apiece.\par \par \uc2 The women, prosecutors said, had recruited vulnerable women to join OneTaste, coerced them into paying for OneTaste's expensive courses, which could run to five figures, and psychologically and sexually abused them.\par \par \uc2 The trial was a striking departure from the organized crime, terrorism and gang-related cases that often play out in Brooklyn's federal courtrooms. Instead of charts showing connections between different members of a crime family, prosecutors displayed diagrams of human anatomy included in OneTaste manuals, as well as visual depictions of orgasmic meditation.\par \par \uc2 ''I can get into more detail if you'd like,'' Christopher Hubbard, a former website manager for the company, said while testifying about its sexual soirees.\par \par \uc2 ''That's OK,'' Sean Fern, a prosecutor, replied.\par \par \uc2 The testimony of more than a half-dozen women who worked for OneTaste was central to the government's case. The women said the company had shaped their identities and that they believed leaving would end their path to enlightenment. Ms. Daedone, Ms. Cherwitz and other members had brainwashed them into thinking there was no life outside of OneTaste, these witnesses said.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Daedone, the witnesses said, had assigned ''research partners'' to practice orgasmic meditation together. Monogamous romantic relationships were discouraged, with OneTaste leaders breaking them up if they thought they were hurting members' spiritual journeys.\par \par \uc2 The witnesses stopped short of accusing Ms. Daedone or Ms. Cherwitz of threatening violence or the loss of property if they left, which defense lawyers seized on. Those who worked for OneTaste had ultimately consented to an alternative lifestyle, the lawyers said, arguing that prosecuting the two women amounted to criminalizing.\par \par \uc2 ''You are not here to determine whether they violated some kind of standard of decency,'' Celia Cohen, a lawyer for Ms. Cherwitz, told the jury during her closing argument.\par \par \uc2 Prosecutors were dealt a major blow before the trial began when they acknowledged that what was expected to be key evidence -- supposed sections from a former OneTaste employee's diaries -- could not be considered authentic. The lead prosecutor, Gillian Kassner, left the case after the evidence was thrown out.\par \par \uc2 At OneTaste, the lines between consensual and forced work, which included sex work, were often blurred, according to prosecution evidence. Tasks ranged from cooking to having sex with OneTaste's wealthiest investors, according to witness testimony. Employees were encouraged to engage in orgasmic meditation with as many people as they could, and mornings at OneTaste's communal homes began with the practice.\par \par \uc2 Several witnesses testified that they had been routinely instructed to pleasure Ms. Daedone's romantic partner sexually, including by having intercourse with him, whipping him and walking him on a leash. Residents of a communal OneTaste house in Harlem cleaned up after Ms. Daedone, who had her own room when she visited while others shared rooms and beds.\par \par \uc2 The women understood at the time, they testified, that such work was part of their jobs, as was engaging in ''OM'' sessions with men they did not find attractive. They said they had agreed to engage in the behavior because they wanted to stay tethered to OneTaste and not run afoul of Ms. Daedone.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Daedone, according to videos shown by prosecutors at the trial, preached that orgasmic meditation could free women from past sexual trauma and that they could avoid such trauma altogether if they were always in the mood for sex.\par \par \uc2 ''The real way to deflect rape is to turn on 100 percent, because then there is nothing to rape,'' Ms. Daedone said at a OneTaste coaching seminar in 2013.\par \par \uc2 At OneTaste's New York headquarters in East Harlem on Saturday, Ms. Daedone projected confidence and defiance while addressing a group of her supporters. Saying her skill in practicing orgasmic meditation made her akin to a ''concert pianist,'' Ms. Daedone said she was a symbol of ''female sexuality and liberation.''\par \par \uc2 She said she had been ''canceled'' by the criminal indictment and that her work had been mischaracterized during the trial.\par \par \uc2 ''The only thing I bow to is free will,'' Ms. Daedone said.\par \par \uc2 In her closing argument, Nina Gupta, a prosecutor, suggested that Ms. Daedone's doctrine was part of the crime. Ms. Daedone, Ms. Gupta said, spread her teachings so ''her employees would only say yes to her.''\par \par \uc2 ''This case is about a group of women who gave everything to these defendants,'' Ms. Gupta said. ''Their money, their time, their bodies, their dignity and, ultimately, their sanity.''\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250610el6a0004k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
