\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 A.I. on Campus: Casting Chatbot As Study Buddy\b0\par\par\uc2 By Natasha Singer \par \uc2 1,512 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 June 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 , the maker of ChatGPT, has a plan to overhaul college education -- by embedding its artificial intelligence tools in every facet of campus life.\par \par \uc2 If the company's strategy succeeds, universities would give students A.I. assistants to help guide and tutor them from orientation day through graduation. Professors would provide customized A.I. study bots for each class. Career services would offer recruiter chatbots for students to practice job interviews. And undergrads could turn on a chatbot's voice mode to be quizzed aloud ahead of a test.\par \par \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  dubs its sales pitch ''A.I.-native universities.''\par \par \uc2 ''Our vision is that, over time, A.I. would become part of the core infrastructure of higher education,'' Leah Belsky, \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 's vice president of education, said in an interview. In the same way that colleges give students school email accounts, she said, soon ''every student who comes to campus would have access to their personalized A.I. account.''\par \par \uc2 To spread chatbots on campuses, \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  is selling premium A.I. services to universities for faculty and student use. It is also running marketing campaigns aimed at getting students who have never used chatbots to try ChatGPT.\par \par \uc2 Some universities, including the University of Maryland and California State University, are already working to make A.I. tools part of students' everyday experiences. In early June, \uc2 Duke University\uc2  began offering unlimited ChatGPT access to students, faculty and staff. The school also introduced a university platform, called DukeGPT, with A.I. tools developed by Duke.\par \par \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 's campaign is part of an escalating A.I. arms race among tech giants to win over universities and students with their chatbots. The company is following in the footsteps of rivals like \uc2 Google\uc2  and \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  that have for years pushed to get their computers and software into schools, and court students as future customers.\par \par \uc2 The competition is so heated that \uc2 Sam Altman\uc2 , \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 's chief executive, and \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , who founded the rival \uc2 xAI\uc2 , posted dueling announcements on social media this spring offering free premium A.I. services for college students during exam period. Then \uc2 Google\uc2  upped the ante, announcing free student access to its premium chatbot service ''through finals 2026.''\par \par \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  ignited the recent A.I. education trend. In late 2022, the company's rollout of ChatGPT, which can produce human-sounding essays and term papers, helped set off a wave of chatbot-fueled cheating. Generative A.I. tools like ChatGPT, which are trained on large databases of texts, also make stuff up, which can mislead students.\par \par \uc2 Less than three years later, millions of college students regularly use A.I. chatbots as research, writing, computer programming and idea-generating aides. Now \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  is capitalizing on ChatGPT's popularity to promote the company's A.I. services to universities as the new infrastructure for college education.\par \par \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 's service for universities, ChatGPT Edu, offers more features, including certain privacy protections, than the company's free chatbot. ChatGPT Edu also enables faculty and staff to create custom chatbots for university use. (\uc2 OpenAI\uc2  offers consumers premium versions of its chatbot for a monthly fee.)\par \par \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 's push to A.I.-ify college education amounts to a national experiment on millions of students. The use of these chatbots in schools is so new that their potential long-term educational benefits, and possible side effects, are not yet established.\par \par \uc2 A few early studies have found that outsourcing tasks like research and writing to chatbots can diminish skills like critical thinking. And some critics argue that colleges going all-in on chatbots are glossing over issues like societal risks, A.I. labor exploitation and environmental costs.\par \par \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 's campus marketing effort comes as unemployment has increased among recent college graduates -- particularly in fields like software engineering, where A.I. is now automating some tasks previously done by humans. In hopes of boosting students' career prospects, some universities are racing to provide A.I. tools and training.\par \par \uc2 California State University announced this year that it was making ChatGPT available to more than 460,000 students across its 23 campuses to help prepare them for ''California's future A.I.-driven economy.'' Cal State said the effort would help make the school ''the nation's first and largest A.I.-empowered university system.''\par \par \uc2 Some universities say they are embracing the new A.I. tools in part because they want their schools to help guide, and develop guardrails for, the technologies.\par \par \uc2 ''You're worried about the ecological concerns. You're worried about misinformation and bias,'' Edmund Clark, the chief information officer of California State University, said at a recent education conference in San Diego. ''Well, join in. Help us shape the future.''\par \par \uc2 Last spring, \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  introduced ChatGPT Edu, its first product for universities, which offers access to the company's latest artificial intelligence. Paying clients like universities also get more privacy: \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  says it does not use the information that students, faculty and administrators enter into ChatGPT Edu to train its A.I.\par \par \uc2 (The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  has sued \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  and its partner, \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 , over copyright infringement. Both companies have denied wrongdoing.)\par \par \uc2 Last fall, \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  hired Ms. Belsky to oversee its education efforts. An ed tech start-up veteran, she previously worked at \uc2 Coursera\uc2 , which offers college and professional training courses.\par \par \uc2 She is pursuing a two-pronged strategy: marketing \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 's premium services to universities for a fee while advertising free ChatGPT directly to students. \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  also convened a panel of college students recently to help get their peers to start using the tech.\par \par \uc2 Among those students are power users like Delphine Tai-Beauchamp, a computer science major at the \uc2 University of California, Irvine\uc2 . She has used the chatbot to explain complicated course concepts, as well as help explain coding errors and make charts diagraming the connections between ideas.\par \par \uc2 ''I wouldn't recommend students use A.I. to avoid the hard parts of learning,'' Ms. Tai-Beauchamp said. She did recommend students try A.I. as a study aid. ''Ask it to explain something five different ways.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Belsky said these kinds of suggestions helped the company create its first billboard campaign aimed at college students.\par \par \uc2 ''Can you quiz me on the muscles of the leg?'' asked one ChatGPT billboard, posted this spring in Chicago. ''Give me a guide for mastering this Calc 101 syllabus,'' another said.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Belsky said \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  had also begun funding research into the educational effects of its chatbots.\par \par \uc2 ''The challenge is, how do you actually identify what are the use cases for A.I. in the university that are most impactful?'' Ms. Belsky said during a December A.I. event at Cornell Tech in New York City. ''And then how do you replicate those best practices across the ecosystem?''\par \par \uc2 Some faculty members have already built custom chatbots for their students by uploading course materials like their lecture notes, slides, videos and quizzes into ChatGPT.\par \par \uc2 Jared DeForest, the chair of environmental and plant biology at \uc2 Ohio University\uc2 , created his own tutoring bot, called SoilSage, which can answer students' questions based on his published research papers and science knowledge. Limiting the chatbot to trusted information sources has improved its accuracy, he said.\par \par \uc2 ''The curated chatbot allows me to control the information in there to get the product that I want at the college level,'' Professor DeForest said.\par \par \uc2 But even when trained on specific course materials, A.I. can make mistakes. In a new study -- ''Can A.I. Hold Office Hours?'' -- law school professors uploaded a patent law casebook into A.I. models from \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 , \uc2 Google\uc2  and \uc2 Anthropic\uc2 . Then they asked dozens of patent law questions based on the casebook and found that all three A.I. chatbots made ''significant'' legal errors that could be ''harmful for learning.''\par \par \uc2 ''This is a good way to lead students astray,'' said Jonathan S. Masur, a professor at the University of Chicago Law School and a co-author of the study. ''So I think that everyone needs to take a little bit of a deep breath and slow down.''\par \par \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  said the 250,000-word casebook used for the study was more than twice the length of text that its GPT-4o model can process at once. \uc2 Anthropic\uc2  said the study had limited usefulness because it did not compare the A.I. with human performance. \uc2 Google\uc2  said its model accuracy had improved since the study was conducted.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Belsky said a new ''memory'' feature, which retains and can refer to previous interactions with a user, would help ChatGPT tailor its responses to students over time and make the A.I. ''more valuable as you grow and learn.''\par \par \uc2 Privacy experts warn that this kind of tracking feature raises concerns about long-term tech company surveillance.\par \par \uc2 In the same way that many students today convert their school-issued Gmail accounts into personal accounts when they graduate, Ms. Belsky envisions graduating students bringing their A.I. chatbots into their workplaces and using them for life.\par \par \uc2 ''It would be their gateway to learning -- and career life thereafter,'' Ms. Belsky said.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250608el680004h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
