\par\par \uc2 STOCKS &AMP; BONDS\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Solid Jobs Report Helps Wall Street Gain Ground\b0\par\par\uc2 By The Associated Press \par \uc2 664 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 June 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Stocks gained ground on Wall Street on Friday following a better-than-expected report on the U.S. job market.\par \par \uc2 The gains were broad, with every sector in the S&P 500 rising. That solidified a second consecutive winning week for the benchmark index, which has rallied back from a slump two months ago to come within striking distance of its record high.\par \par \uc2 The S&P 500 rose 61.06 points, or 1 percent, to 6,000.36. It is now within 2.3 percent of its record.\par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones industrial average rose 443.13 points, or 1 percent, to 42,762.87. The Nasdaq rose 231.50 points, or 1.2 percent, to 19,529.95.\par \par \uc2 Technology stocks, with their outsized values, led the broad gains. \uc2 Nvidia\uc2  jumped 1.2 percent and \uc2 Apple\uc2  rose 1.6 percent.\par \par \uc2 Tesla\uc2  rose 3.7 percent, regaining some of the big losses that it suffered on Thursday when President Trump and \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  sparred feverishly on social media.\par \par \uc2 Circle Internet Group\uc2 , the U.S.-based issuer of one of the most popular cryptocurrencies, rose 29.4 percent. That adds to its 168 percent gain from Thursday when it debuted on the New York Stock Exchange.\par \par \uc2 U.S. employers slowed their hiring last month, but still added a solid 139,000 jobs amid uncertainty over Mr. Trump's trade war. The closely watched monthly update reaffirmed that the job market remains resilient, despite worries from businesses and consumers about the impact of tariffs on goods going to and coming from the U.S. and its most important trading partners.\par \par \uc2 ''It looks like, for now, everything is kind of running smoothly,'' said \uc2 Chris Zaccarelli\uc2 , chief investment officer for Northlight Asset Management. ''Investors see that as a positive, but we also haven't seen the full effect of tariffs yet.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump's on-again-off-again tariffs continue to weigh on companies. \uc2 Lululemon Athletica\uc2  plunged 19.8 percent after the maker of yoga clothing cut its profit expectations late Thursday as it tries to offset the impact of tariffs while being buffeted by competition from start-up brands.\par \par \uc2 Lululemon\uc2  joins a wide range of companies, from retailers to airlines, who have warned investors about the potential hit to their revenue and profits because of tariffs raising costs and consumers potentially tightening spending.\par \par \uc2 Hopes that Mr. Trump will lower his tariffs after reaching trade deals with other countries have been among the main reasons the S&P 500 has rallied back so furiously since dropping roughly 20 percent from its record two months ago. Senior U.S. administration officials will meet with a Chinese delegation on Monday in London for the next round of trade negotiations between Washington and Beijing.\par \par \uc2 The economy is already absorbing the impact from tariffs on a wide range of goods from key trading partners, along with raw materials such as steel. Heavier tariffs could hit businesses and consumers in the coming months.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. economy contracted during the first quarter. Recent surveys by the \uc2 Institute for Supply Management\uc2 , a trade group of purchasing managers, found that both American manufacturing and services businesses contracted last month. On Tuesday, the \uc2 Organization for Economic Cooperation and Development\uc2  forecast 1.6 percent growth for the U.S. economy this year, down from 2.8 percent last year.\par \par \uc2 The central bank is holding its benchmark interest rate steady as it worries about tariffs reigniting inflation. It fought hard, using interest rate increases, to ease inflation back toward its target of 2 percent and rates have been hovering just above that level.\par \par \uc2 The Fed has been hesitant to cut interest rates in 2025 after trimming rates three times late last year. While lower interest rates can give the economy a boost, they can also push inflation higher. That could be especially damaging if import taxes are also raising costs for businesses and consumers.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250607el670004j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
